export const explanations: Record<string, string> = {
  'n3-own-b1-01': 'Answer: <b>わけがない</b>. The first half states a premise as fact, 「あんなに 正直な」 (someone that honest), and the second half draws a strong negative inference from it: <b>“there is no way.”</b><br>Distractors: <b>わけだ</b> means “no wonder / that means” (explaining a result); <b>ことになる</b> is an objective decision or outcome; <b>ものだ</b> expresses common sense or a feeling.<br>How to solve it: <b>first check whether the first half gives a fact to base a judgment on</b>. If it does, the second half is most likely an inference from that fact, such as わけがない.',
  'n3-own-b1-02': 'Answer: <b>静かなわけがない</b>. When a na-adjective takes わけがない, keep the <b>な</b>.<br>Learn the connections for each word class as a set:<br>・<b>Verbs and i-adjectives</b>: plain form, attached directly (行くわけがない／行かないわけがない／高いわけがない)<br>・<b>Na-adjectives</b>: + <b>な</b> (静か<u>な</u>わけがない)<br>・<b>Nouns</b>: + <b>の</b> (彼が 犯人<u>の</u>わけがない)<br>The distractors drop the な, attach だ directly, or wrongly use the noun pattern with の.<br>Note that verbs are not limited to the dictionary form: <b>the nai-form and ta-form are plain forms too, and connect the same way</b>.',
  'n3-own-b1-03': 'Answer: <b>わけではない</b> = “it\'s not that I can\'t speak it at all.” The second half goes on to say “but I\'m not confident yet,” so the first half must be a <b>partial negation</b>, not a total one.<br><b>This is where most points are lost on this grammar point</b>: a small difference in form makes a big difference in meaning.<br>・<b>わけがない</b> = <b>impossible</b> (strong negative inference)<br>・<b>わけではない</b> = <b>not entirely…</b> (partial negation, leaves room)<br>If you choose わけがない, the sentence becomes “there is no way I can\'t speak Japanese, but I\'m not confident,” which contradicts itself.<br>Other distractors: <b>わけにはいかない</b> is “cannot do it (because of duty or one\'s position)”; <b>わけだ</b> is “no wonder.”',
  'n3-own-b1-04': 'Answer: <b>に違いない</b>. From the <b>visible sign</b> that the light is off, the speaker concludes “they must have gone to bed.”<br>Distractors: <b>わけではない</b> is a partial negation; <b>ことにする</b> is your own decision; <b>ものだ</b> is common sense or a feeling.<br>The hallmark of this pattern is <b>a piece of observable evidence before it</b>: the light is off, the shoes are gone, the light is still on. When you see that kind of setup, think of に違いない.',
  'n3-own-b1-05': 'Answer: <b>に違いない</b>. <b>Nouns and na-adjectives attach directly</b>, with nothing in between.<br>The distractors add だ, の or な. Of these, <b>のに違いない</b> does exist as a form (彼は 知って いた<b>のに</b>違いない, where の is a nominalizer). A noun can also take の, but then that の means <b>“the one belonging to”</b>: 「この かばんは 先生<b>の</b>に違いない」 = “this bag must be <b>the teacher\'s</b>.” Here the subject is 「あの 人」, so 「あの人は 先生のに違いない」 would mean “that person must be the teacher\'s (thing),” which makes no sense. To say “must be a teacher,” only 「先生に違いない」 works (in writing, 「先生であるに違いない」 is also possible).<br>Summary: <b>plain form + に違いない</b>, but for nouns and na-adjectives <b>drop だ and attach directly</b>.<br>　・行く<b>に違いない</b>／高い<b>に違いない</b>／静か<b>に違いない</b>／先生<b>に違いない</b>',
  'n3-own-b1-06': 'Answer: <b>に違いない → だろう → かもしれない</b>.<br>・<b>に違いない</b> = nearly certain, “must be.” Use it only when you have concrete grounds.<br>・<b>だろう</b> = an ordinary guess, “probably.”<br>・<b>かもしれない</b> = one possibility, “might.” You can use it even when you are less than 50% sure.<br>Choosing the wrong one in writing or speech throws off your tone: に違いない without grounds sounds overbearing, and かもしれない when you are sure sounds unconfident.<br>Also note that <b>に違いない is the speaker\'s own judgment</b>. You cannot use it to report what someone else said (for that, use 〜そうだ／〜とのことだ).',
  'n3-own-b1-07': 'Answer: <b>切り</b> (走り切る). The two clues <b>「つらかったが」 + 「42キロを」</b> point to <b>completion</b>: the runner made it through the whole course.<br>The distractors are other compound-verb endings: <b>走り出す</b> (break into a run), <b>走り始める</b> (begin running), and <b>走りかける</b> = start running but not finish (かける marks an action that has <b>begun but is not complete</b>: 読みかけの 本・食べかけの パン). 「彼は 走りかけて、立ち止まった」 = he had barely started running when he stopped, <b>the exact opposite of 走り切る (keep going to the end)</b>. 出す／始める are about the start and かける is about not finishing, so all three are the opposite of seeing it through.<br>The difference from 「走り終わる」 is nuance, not grammar: <b>終わる only says the action ended, while 切る carries a sense of achievement, of having made it</b>. This sentence has 「つらかったが」, so 終わる would erase that sense of effort.',
  'n3-own-b1-08': 'Answer: <b>使い切る</b>. Compound-verb endings always attach to <b>the masu-stem (the masu-form minus ます)</b>: 使い<u>ます</u> → 使い + 切る.<br>The distractors use the dictionary form, the te-form and the ta-form. None of these works in a compound verb.<br>The same rule applies to the whole group: <b>走り切る・言い切る・書き直す・考え直す・し始める・食べ続ける</b>. <b>Change to the masu-form, then drop ます</b>: that is the general rule for this type.',
  'n3-own-b1-09': 'Answer: <b>「ぜんぶ つかって、のこって いない」</b>. The core of 〜切る is <b>“to the limit, nothing left,”</b> so 「使い切った」 means the money has all been spent.<br>The distractor 「つかうのを とちゅうで やめた」 would be 「使いかけた／使うのをやめた」; the distractor 「つかい方を まちがえた」 has nothing to do with this pattern.<br>The same logic extends to other words: <b>売り切れる</b> (sell out, none left), <b>疲れ切る</b> (be utterly exhausted), <b>言い切る</b> (state flatly, assert).<br>When you see 〜切る, think “<b>all the way</b>,” whether that means finishing something or using it up.',
  'n3-own-b1-10': 'Answer: <b>直し</b> (書き直す). <b>「まちがいが 多かったので」</b> is the premise: the first attempt was not good enough, so it was done again.<br>Distractors: <b>書き切る</b> is write to the end (nothing left); <b>書き始める</b> is start writing; <b>書き終わる</b> is finish writing. None of them means “fix.”<br>The key to 〜直す is that <b>it assumes the previous attempt was not good enough</b>. When the sentence sets up 「まちがい」, 「うまくいかなかった」, 「もう一度」 and the like, think of this pattern.',
  'n3-own-b1-11': 'Answer: <b>作り直し</b>. The size was given wrongly, so the suit already made is not right, and they <b>make a new one</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>作り切る</b> (make to the end), <b>作り始める</b> (start making) and <b>作り続ける</b> (keep making) have no sense of fixing anything.<br>This group comes up often in conversation, so learn it as a set:<br>　・<b>やり直す</b> (do over)・<b>考え直す</b> (reconsider)・<b>読み直す</b> (reread to check)・<b>見直す</b> (review / see in a new light)<br>(Why a made-to-order setting: when a ready-made suit doesn\'t fit, the shop says 「お直しします」 = we\'ll alter it; <b>作り直す</b> = remake the whole thing, which only makes sense for something made to order.)',
  'n3-own-b1-12': 'Answer: <b>「読み直す＝もう一度 読む、読み終わる＝読むのが 終わる」</b>.<br>・<b>読み直す</b> = read again (because you didn\'t understand, or to check for mistakes)<br>・<b>読み終わる</b> = finish reading (simply states that the action ended)<br>Both attach to the masu-stem and look alike, but <b>直す assumes the last attempt was not good enough, while 終わる only reports that the action ended</b>.<br>This group is easier to remember as directions:<br>　<b>〜始める</b> (start) → <b>〜続ける</b> (continue) → <b>〜終わる</b> (end) ／ <b>〜切る</b> (see it through) ／ <b>〜直す</b> (do it over)',
  'n3-own-b1-13': 'Answer: <b>恐れが</b>. 「〜恐れがある」 is a set phrase in <b>news and formal warnings</b>, expressing concern that something bad may happen.<br>Distractors: <b>ことが</b> makes 「〜ことがある」 = “sometimes happens,” which describes how often something happens <b>at unspecified times</b> and goes with general time expressions like 「大雨の ときは」 (「この 川は 大雨の とき、水が あふれる ことが あります」 is perfectly correct on its own). But this question pins the time to <b>a specific future period</b> with 「今夜から あすの 朝に かけて」. It is a risk forecast for this one occasion, so a general frequency sentence doesn\'t fit. <b>ものが</b> and <b>わけが</b> don\'t form a sentence in this context.<br>The register of this pattern is very fixed: <b>weather reports, medicine leaflets, public notices</b>. When you see a written-style setup such as 「〜により」 or 「〜から〜にかけて」, 恐れがある is very likely to follow.',
  'n3-own-b1-14': 'Answer (the incorrect usage): <b>「あしたは すばらしい 天気に なる 恐れが あります。」</b>.<br>「恐れがある」 <b>can only be used for negative or dangerous things</b>. Good weather is a good thing, so pairing it with 恐れ (fear, concern) is a direct contradiction. For the possibility of something good, use 「〜でしょう」 or 「〜かもしれません」.<br>The other three are typical correct uses: a typhoon making landfall, side effects of a medicine, a building collapsing. All are <b>things you don\'t want to happen</b>.<br>The approach here is simple: <b>first ask whether the event is good or bad</b>. If it\'s good, rule it out right away.',
  'n3-own-b1-15': 'Answer: <b>の</b>. <b>When a noun takes 「恐れがある」, link them with の</b>: 事故<u>の</u>恐れがある.<br>Distractors: が／に／を cannot follow a noun and then connect to 恐れ.<br>Learn the two connections as a set:<br>　・<b>verb dictionary form + 恐れがある</b> (あふれる恐れがある)<br>　・<b>noun + の + 恐れがある</b> (事故の恐れがある)<br>This is the same rule as 「〜の間」 and 「〜のために」: <b>a noun that modifies a noun takes の</b>. It is not a special rule of this pattern.',
  'n3-own-b1-16': 'Answer: <b>に沿って</b>. It means moving along <b>something linear</b>, such as a road, a river or a coastline.<br>The three distractors are all “about / toward” patterns, and they make no sense after 「道」: <b>に関して</b> (a discussion about this road), <b>について</b> (same), <b>に対して</b> (taking an attitude toward this road).<br>The four look alike but work completely differently: <b>に沿って means “following” a space or a policy, while the other three mark a topic or a target</b>.',
  'n3-own-b1-17': 'Answer: <b>に沿って</b>. This is the <b>abstract use</b> of the same expression: proceeding in line with a policy, plan or rule.<br>Distractors: <b>によって</b> marks a means or the agent of an action; <b>に比べて</b> is comparison; <b>にとって</b> is a standpoint, “for (someone).”<br>Worth remembering: <b>“walk along the river” and “follow the plan” use the same expression in Japanese</b>. The meaning extends from physical space to the abstract, so these are not two separate uses to memorize. Even though English often splits them into “along” and “in line with / according to,” you can connect both through the idea of “following.”',
  'n3-own-b1-18': 'Answer: <b>に沿って</b>. The hotels are lined up <b>along the coastline</b>, a typical spatial use.<br>Distractors: <b>に関する</b>／<b>に対する</b> are attributive forms and must be followed directly by a noun (海岸に関する規則), not by a clause like 「ホテルが ならんで います」; <b>によると</b> gives a source of hearsay (天気予報によると).<br>The hint is in the verb: <b>actions that extend along a line, such as 「ならぶ・つづく・歩く・走る」</b>, usually pair with に沿って.',
  'n3-own-b1-19': 'Answer: <b>関する</b>. When a noun (本) follows directly, use the <b>attributive form 「に関する + noun」</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>に関して</b> is the te-form and must be followed by <b>a verb phrase</b> (この件に関して 説明します). It cannot modify a noun directly, so 「環境問題に関して 本です」 is not a sentence. <b>関し</b> is a stiffer written continuative form and is not normally used this way; <b>関され</b> is not a word.<br>Learn them as a pair:<br>　・<b>Nに関して + verb</b> (この件に関して 話し合う)<br>　・<b>Nに関する + noun</b> (この件に関する 資料)<br>To decide, just look at <b>whether a noun or a verb follows the blank</b>.',
  'n3-own-b1-20': 'Answer: <b>に関して</b>. A verb phrase follows (ご説明いたします), so use the <b>te-form</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>に関する</b> must be followed by a noun; <b>に沿って</b> is “along / in line with”; <b>にわたって</b> is “over (a period or range).”<br>The sentence uses humble language, 「ご説明いたします」, so it is <b>formal in style</b>, which is exactly where に関して is common. In casual speech you would say 「その件<b>について</b>」. <b>に関して is more formal than について</b> and appears mostly in meetings, reports and business letters.',
  'n3-own-b1-21': 'Answer: <b>「いみは ほぼ 同じだが、に関して の ほうが あらたまった 場面で つかう」</b>. The two mean almost the same thing; the difference is <b>register</b>:<br>・<b>について</b>: general, common in speech too (日本について 話す)<br>・<b>に関して</b>: formal, written (本件に関して ご報告します)<br>Meeting minutes, official documents and research reports mostly use に関して. When chatting with friends, について is fine, and に関して would sound stiff.<br>The other options treat it as a difference of place vs time or affirmative vs negative, which is a completely different grammatical axis. <b>The only difference in this pair is how formal it sounds</b>.',
  'n3-own-b1-22': 'Answer: <b>とともに</b>. <b>Two things change together</b>: as the times change, ways of thinking change too.<br>Distractors: <b>かわりに</b> is substitution or exchange; <b>おかげで</b>／<b>せいで</b> express cause (with a positive and a negative evaluation respectively), but this sentence only describes parallel change, with no judgment of good or bad.<br>Rule of thumb: <b>when both halves have change verbs like 「変わる・増える・進む」 and there is no praise or blame</b>, it is usually とともに.',
  'n3-own-b1-23': 'Answer: <b>家族と いっしょに</b>.<br>とともに has two uses, and this question tests the <b>doing-it-together</b> one:<br>　・<b>Together</b>: 家族<b>とともに</b> 暮らす (= と一緒に, more formal)<br>　・<b>Accompanying change</b>: 時代が 変わる<b>とともに</b> (= as …)<br>How to tell them apart: <b>check whether what comes before is a person or a change</b>. After a person it means “together with”; after a verb or noun of change it means “as.”<br>The distractors match other patterns: かわりに (instead of), ために (for the sake of), にくらべて (compared with).',
  'n3-own-b1-24': 'Answer: <b>とともに</b>. Growing older and eyesight getting worse <b>happen in parallel</b>, a typical sentence of accompanying change.<br>Distractors: <b>ものだから</b> gives a reason as an excuse (“because…, so please understand”); <b>わけで</b> draws a conclusion, “that is to say”; <b>ばかりに</b> is “just because… (it led to a bad result),” with a tone of regret.<br><b>につれて</b> (as, gradually) would also work here, and the two are almost interchangeable in this context. The difference: <b>とともに is more formal, while につれて puts more stress on the gradual process</b>.',
  'n3-own-b1-25': 'Answer: <b>ために</b>. This is the <b>cause</b> use: because of the accident, the train stopped.<br>Check with the relationship test: 「電車が 止まりました」 was not done <b>to make the accident happen</b>. The accident happened first and the train stopped as a result, so it\'s cause. Also, the second half is a non-volitional event (電車が 止まった), which matches a hard clue.<br>The test looks at <b>the relationship between the two events</b>, not at what the first one is. The same word can carry both meanings: 「試験の ために 毎日 勉強する」 (purpose) ／ 「試験の ために 学校は 休みだ」 (cause), and both start with 「試験」.<br>Ask one question: <b>was the second action done to make the first thing happen, or for the good of the person named first?</b><br>　・Yes → <b>purpose</b> (家を 買う ために 貯金する／健康の ために 走る／子どもの ために 働く)<br>　・No, the first thing happened and the second followed → <b>cause</b> (事故の ために 電車が 止まった／病気の ために 会社を 休んだ)<br>Substitution test: if 「〜が 原因で」 keeps the meaning, it\'s cause; if 「〜を 目指して」 keeps the meaning, it\'s purpose.<br>There are also two <b>formal hard clues</b> that settle it at once: <b>ta-form, te-iru form, i-adjective or na-adjective + ために is always cause</b> (事故が 起きた ために／道が 狭い ために); and <b>if the second half is a non-volitional event</b> (〜が 止まった・遅れた・中止に なった), <b>it is also always cause</b>. But <b>the reverse does not hold</b>: a volitional action in the second half does not mean purpose (the 「休んだ」 in 「病気の ために 休んだ」 is something you chose to do, yet it is still cause).<br>Distractors: <b>ように</b> expresses a hoped-for goal (after a potential or negative form); <b>とおりに</b> is “just as…”; <b>ばかりに</b> is “just because… (things ended up badly).”',
  'n3-own-b1-26': 'Answer: <b>原因</b> (cause). They were sick, so they took the day off.<br>Check with the relationship test: <b>taking the day off was not done “to make the illness happen.”</b> The illness came first and the day off followed, so it\'s cause.<br>The test looks at <b>the relationship between the two events</b>, not at what the first one is. The same word can carry both meanings: 「試験の ために 毎日 勉強する」 (purpose) ／ 「試験の ために 学校は 休みだ」 (cause), and both start with 「試験」.<br>Ask one question: <b>was the second action done to make the first thing happen, or for the good of the person named first?</b><br>　・Yes → <b>purpose</b> (家を 買う ために 貯金する／健康の ために 走る／子どもの ために 働く)<br>　・No, the first thing happened and the second followed → <b>cause</b> (事故の ために 電車が 止まった／病気の ために 会社を 休んだ)<br>Substitution test: if 「〜が 原因で」 keeps the meaning, it\'s cause; if 「〜を 目指して」 keeps the meaning, it\'s purpose.<br>There are also two <b>formal hard clues</b> that settle it at once: <b>ta-form, te-iru form, i-adjective or na-adjective + ために is always cause</b> (事故が 起きた ために／道が 狭い ために); and <b>if the second half is a non-volitional event</b> (〜が 止まった・遅れた・中止に なった), <b>it is also always cause</b>. But <b>the reverse does not hold</b>: a volitional action in the second half does not mean purpose (the 「休んだ」 in 「病気の ために 休んだ」 is something you chose to do, yet it is still cause).<br>This question shows exactly why you can\'t look only at the second half: 「会社を 休んだ」 is an action you chose, so if you reasoned “a volitional second half means purpose,” you would get it wrong.',
  'n3-own-b1-27': 'Answer: <b>「多くは 意志の ない できごと」</b>. In the cause use, the second half is <b>usually</b> a non-volitional event: 電車が 止まった, 飛行機が 遅れた, 店が 閉まった.<br>This direction is reliable: <b>non-volitional second half ⇒ always cause</b>. But <b>the reverse does not hold</b>: a volitional action in the second half does not mean purpose (the 「休んだ」 in 「病気の ために 会社を 休んだ」 is something you chose to do, yet it is still cause). So the distractor 「<b>かならず</b> 話す人が 自分で 決めた 行動」 is wrong because it turns a one-way clue into a two-way rule, and adds 「かならず」 (always) on top.<br>The test looks at <b>the relationship between the two events</b>, not at what the first one is. The same word can carry both meanings: 「試験の ために 毎日 勉強する」 (purpose) ／ 「試験の ために 学校は 休みだ」 (cause), and both start with 「試験」.<br>Ask one question: <b>was the second action done to make the first thing happen, or for the good of the person named first?</b><br>　・Yes → <b>purpose</b> (家を 買う ために 貯金する／健康の ために 走る／子どもの ために 働く)<br>　・No, the first thing happened and the second followed → <b>cause</b> (事故の ために 電車が 止まった／病気の ために 会社を 休んだ)<br>Substitution test: if 「〜が 原因で」 keeps the meaning, it\'s cause; if 「〜を 目指して」 keeps the meaning, it\'s purpose.<br>There are also two <b>formal hard clues</b> that settle it at once: <b>ta-form, te-iru form, i-adjective or na-adjective + ために is always cause</b> (事故が 起きた ために／道が 狭い ために); and <b>if the second half is a non-volitional event</b> (〜が 止まった・遅れた・中止に なった), <b>it is also always cause</b>. But <b>the reverse does not hold</b>: a volitional action in the second half does not mean purpose (the 「休んだ」 in 「病気の ために 休んだ」 is something you chose to do, yet it is still cause).',
  'n3-own-b1-28': 'Answer: <b>おかげで</b>. The result is passing the exam, <b>a good thing</b>, and you want to thank the person, so おかげで fits.<br>Distractors: <b>せいで</b> blames someone for a bad result; here it would mean blaming the teacher for making you pass, which is contradictory. <b>くせに</b> is a reproachful “even though.”<br><b>ために</b> doesn\'t work, for two reasons. ① <b>ために has no built-in sense of “thanks to” or gratitude</b>, so even read as a cause it can\'t express the thanks this sentence needs. ② At N3 level, 「先生の ために」 is almost always read as <b>“for the teacher\'s sake (I passed).”</b> Person or organization + のために usually means benefit; to say “because of someone (something bad happened),” Japanese normally uses 「〜のせいで」.<br>Learn them as a pair: <b>おかげで (good result, gratitude)</b> ／ <b>せいで (bad result, complaint)</b>. <b>Check first whether the result is good or bad, and you can choose between the two</b>.',
  'n3-own-b1-29': 'Answer: <b>おかげで</b>. <b>A verb in plain form can attach directly to おかげで</b>: 手伝って くれた<u>おかげで</u>.<br>The distractors swap the particle or use a sentence-ending form. <b>おかげで</b> is the form that links two clauses, so in the middle of a sentence you need で.<br>Connections:<br>　・<b>verb / adjective plain form + おかげで</b> (合格できたおかげで)<br>　・<b>noun + の + おかげで</b> (先生<u>の</u>おかげで)<br>　・At the end of a sentence on its own, it is <b>「〜おかげです」</b> (合格できたのは 先生の おかげです)',
  'n3-own-b1-30': 'Answer (the incorrect usage): <b>「道が こんで いた おかげで、会議に 遅れて しまった。」</b>. 「遅れて しまった」 is a <b>bad result</b>, so it should be <b>せいで</b>: 道が こんで いた<b>せいで</b>、会議に 遅れて しまった.<br>The other three all have good results: the photos came out well, a good job was found, the fever went down.<br>One exception: <b>おかげで is sometimes used with a bad result for sarcasm</b> (「君の おかげで ひどい 目に あったよ」 = thanks a lot, it\'s all your doing), but that is deliberate irony, and <b>the N3 exam won\'t test it that way</b>. If the result is bad, choose せいで.',
  'n3-own-b2-01': 'Answer: <b>せいで</b>. The result is a headache, <b>a bad thing</b>, said with a complaining tone.<br>Distractors: <b>おかげで</b> is for good results and gratitude, so it contradicts the headache; <b>ように</b> is a hoped-for goal; <b>とおりに</b> is “just as….”<br>Learn the pair firmly: <b>おかげで (good result) ／ せいで (bad result)</b>. <b>Decide first whether the second half is good or bad, and you can choose between the two</b>.',
  'n3-own-b2-02': 'Answer: <b>の</b>. <b>When a noun takes せいで, link them with の</b>: 雨<u>の</u>せいで.<br>Connections (exactly the same as おかげで):<br>　・<b>noun + の + せいで</b> (雨のせいで)<br>　・<b>verb / adjective plain form + せいで</b> (寝坊したせいで)<br>　・At the end of a sentence on its own, it is <b>「〜せいです」</b> (中止に なったのは 雨の せいです)<br>This is the same rule as 「〜のために」 and 「〜の間」: <b>a noun that modifies a noun takes の</b>.',
  'n3-own-b2-03': 'Answer (the incorrect usage): <b>「先生が 教えて くれた せいで、テストが よく できました。」</b>. 「テストが よく できました」 is a <b>good result</b>, and a moment to thank the teacher, so it should be <b>おかげで</b>.<br>Using せいで amounts to saying “it\'s the teacher\'s fault for teaching me, and now I did well on the test,” which turns thanks into a complaint.<br>The other three all have bad results: being late, not being able to sleep, causing everyone trouble.<br>The approach is simple: <b>look at the result in the second half, and use おかげで for good things and せいで for bad ones</b>.',
  'n3-own-b2-04': 'Answer: <b>の代わりに</b>. <b>When a noun takes 代わりに, use の</b>: 店長<u>の</u>代わりに = in place of the manager.<br>The distractors replace の with に／を／が, and none of them works.<br>Connections:<br>　・<b>noun + の + 代わりに</b> (店長の代わりに)<br>　・<b>verb dictionary form + 代わりに</b> (教える代わりに)<br>By the way, 「店長<b>に代わって</b>」 also exists. It means the same thing but uses a verb in the te-form, and both are correct. But <b>「に代わりに」 is neither of them</b>.',
  'n3-own-b2-05': 'Answer: <b>代わりに</b>. This is its <b>second use, an exchange</b>: I teach you Japanese, and in return you teach me English.<br>Distractors: <b>ために</b> is purpose or cause; <b>おかげで</b> is cause with gratitude; <b>ながら</b> is doing two actions at once (教えながら = while teaching…), which makes no sense here.<br>Learn the two uses as a set:<br>　・<b>Substitution</b>: 店長<b>の</b>代わりに 出席する (do it in someone\'s place)<br>　・<b>Exchange</b>: 教える 代わりに 教えて もらう (in return)<br>How to tell them apart: <b>check for a give-and-take between equals</b>. If there is one, it\'s an exchange.',
  'n3-own-b2-06': 'Answer: <b>「だれかの 代理を する ／ 交換の 条件を あらわす」</b>.<br>・<b>Substitution</b>: 父の 代わりに 私が 行きます。(The person who should go can\'t, so I step in.)<br>・<b>Exchange</b>: 手伝う 代わりに、ごはんを おごって。(I\'ll help, and you buy me a meal.)<br>The shared core is <b>“swap A for B”</b>: one swaps a person, the other swaps a condition.<br>The distractors list the functions of other patterns (cause and effect, time and place, affirmative and negative), which have nothing to do with this one.',
  'n3-own-b2-07': 'Answer: <b>とのことです</b>. It <b>passes on news that someone else gave you</b>, and often appears at the end of emails, notices and announcements.<br>Distractors: <b>に違いありません</b> is the speaker\'s own conclusion, not reported information; <b>ことにします</b> is your own decision; <b>わけがありません</b> is a strong denial.<br>The key distinction: <b>with とのことだ the information comes from someone else; with に違いない the judgment is your own</b>. When the setting is an email, a notice or 「〜によると」, think hearsay.',
  'n3-own-b2-08': 'Answer: <b>「いみは ほぼ 同じだが、とのことだ の ほうが あらたまった 場面で つかう」</b>. Both report hearsay; the difference is <b>register</b>:<br>・<b>〜そうです</b>: general, spoken (雨が 降るそうです)<br>・<b>〜とのことです</b>: formal, written (雨が 降るとのことです)<br>Emails, notices and business messages mostly use とのことです; with friends, そうです is fine.<br>The distractor 「とのことだ は 自分の 考え、そうだ は 人から 聞いた こと」 gets it backwards: <b>both are things you heard</b>, and neither is your own idea (your own judgment would be に違いない／だろう).',
  'n3-own-b2-09': 'Answer: <b>によると</b>. <b>「〜によると」 marks the source of hearsay</b> and forms a fixed pair with endings like 「〜ということです／〜そうです」.<br>Distractors: <b>について</b>／<b>に対して</b> mark a topic or a target; <b>に沿って</b> is “along / in line with.”<br>Learn the fixed combination as a set:<br>　<b>[source]によると + [content] + ということです／とのことです／そうです</b><br>If によると opens the sentence, the ending is almost always a hearsay form, and the reverse is also true. This is a quick clue for finding the answer.',
  'n3-own-b2-10': 'Answer: <b>につれて</b>. Getting better at Japanese and studying becoming fun happen <b>together and gradually</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>によって</b> is a means or an agent; <b>に対して</b> is a target or a contrast; <b>にとって</b> is a standpoint, “for (someone).”<br>The hallmark of this pattern is <b>verbs of gradual change in both halves</b> (〜になる・〜ていく・〜てくる). The sentence often ends with 「〜てきた／〜ていく」, because it describes a process, not a result.',
  'n3-own-b2-11': 'Answer: <b>たつ</b>. <b>につれて follows the verb dictionary form</b>: 時間が 経<u>つ</u>につれて.<br>The distractors are the ta-form, the te-form and the masu-stem, and none of them can take につれて.<br>Connections:<br>　・<b>verb dictionary form + につれて</b> (年を とるにつれて)<br>　・<b>noun of change + につれて</b> (時代の 変化<u>に</u>つれて)<br>Note that <b>it does not take the ta-form</b>. This pattern describes <b>an ongoing process</b>, and the past tense would present the process as finished, which doesn\'t fit.',
  'n3-own-b2-12': 'Answer: <b>にしたがって</b>. This is about <b>following instructions or rules</b>, not natural gradual change.<br><b>This pair is one of the most confused at N3</b>:<br>　・<b>につれて</b> = along with natural gradual change (年を とるにつれて 目が 悪く なる): <b>no one is directing it</b><br>　・<b>にしたがって</b> = following rules, instructions or steps (指示にしたがって 進む): <b>there is something to follow</b><br>How to tell them apart: <b>ask “did the first thing happen naturally, or is a person or a rule requiring it?”</b> If it happens naturally, use につれて; if there is a rule to follow, use にしたがって.<br>(にしたがって can also express natural gradual change, and the two are often interchangeable, but <b>after 「指示・規則・命令」 only にしたがって works</b>.)',
  'n3-own-b2-13': 'Answer: <b>しかない</b>. The last train is gone = <b>there is no other way</b>, so you have to walk home.<br>All three distractors are grammatical, so only the context rules them out:<br>・<b>歩いて 帰ることが ある</b> = “I sometimes walk home.” That is a repeated habit, but this is a sudden situation <b>this one time</b>.<br>・<b>歩いて 帰るはずが ない</b> = concluding from reason that “it can\'t be so.” Its subject can be yourself (私が 知っているはずが ない), but it judges <b>whether a statement is true</b> and is not used to announce what you will do next, so it doesn\'t fit here.<br>・<b>歩いて 帰るわけには いかない</b> = <b>cannot</b> do it (because of some concern or principle). <b>It does not mean “there is no other way”</b>: in 「明日は 試験だから 休むわけには いかない」 the point is not a lack of options; taking the day off just isn\'t acceptable. This question says 「家までは 歩いて 20分だ」 and gives no reason why walking is not allowed, so it doesn\'t work.<br>Also keep the nuance apart from <b>ことにする</b>:<br>　・<b>歩いて 帰るしかない</b> = <b>have no choice but</b> to walk home (resigned, no options)<br>　・<b>歩いて 帰ることに した</b> = <b>decided</b> to walk home (your own choice)<br>After a setup like 「〜てしまった」 or 「〜がなくなった」, the situation has forced you into it, so use しかない.',
  'n3-own-b2-14': 'Answer: <b>待つしかない</b>. <b>しかない follows the verb dictionary form</b>.<br>The distractors are the masu-stem, the te-form and the nai-stem, and none of them works.<br>Also note: <b>しか itself always pairs with a negative</b> (一つしか ない = there is only one), and 「〜しかない」 extends that rule: “there is no option other than doing this.” So it <b>cannot be made affirmative</b>; there is no such form as 「〜しかある」.',
  'n3-own-b2-15': 'Answer: <b>「ほかに 方法が ないので、やるほか ない」</b>. The core of しかない is <b>“no other choice”</b>: not wanting to do it, but having no other way.<br>The distractor 「やりたくて たまらない」 reads it as a strong wish; 「やるかどうか まだ 決めて いない」 means still undecided; 「やらなくても いい」 means the opposite.<br>Synonyms: <b>「〜ほかない」「〜よりほかない」</b>, which sound more formal.<br>Conversely, if the sentence means you actively chose this path, use <b>〜ことにする</b>. <b>The action may be the same, but the feeling is completely different</b>.',
  'n3-own-b2-16': 'Answer: <b>に対して</b>. This is its <b>first use: an attitude or action directed at someone</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>に沿って</b> is “along / in line with”; <b>につれて</b> is “as (gradually)”; <b>にとって</b> is an evaluation from someone\'s standpoint, “for (someone)” (お客様にとって 便利だ = convenient from the customer\'s point of view).<br><b>に対して and にとって are the easiest to confuse</b>:<br>　・<b>に対して</b> = the action is <b>directed at</b> that person (客に対して 話す)<br>　・<b>にとって</b> = judged from that person\'s <b>point of view</b> (客にとって 便利だ)',
  'n3-own-b2-17': 'Answer: <b>のに</b>. This is the <b>second use, contrast</b>. After a plain form it needs an extra <b>の</b>: 活発な<u>の</u>に対して.<br>Connections (for contrast, watch the word class):<br>　・<b>verb / i-adjective plain form + のに対して</b> (よく 話す<u>の</u>に対して)<br>　・<b>na-adjective + な + のに対して</b> (活発<b>な</b><u>の</u>に対して)<br>　・<b>noun + な／である + のに対して</b> (兄は 学生<b>な</b><u>の</u>に対して…)<br>　・(target use) <b>noun + に対して</b> (お客様に対して)<br>The plain form of a na-adjective is 「活発<b>だ</b>」, and before のに対して the だ changes to <b>な</b>. So it is <b>「活発なのに対して」</b>, and you need both な and の. The same goes for nouns: you can\'t write 「学生だのに対して」.<br>Note that this 「のに」 is <b>not</b> the concessive 「のに」 (〜のに = even though…). It is just the nominalizing の + に.',
  'n3-own-b2-18': 'Answer: <b>「ある 相手への たいど ／ 二つの ことの 対比」</b>.<br>・<b>Target</b>: 先生<b>に対して</b> 失礼な ことを 言った。(The action is directed at someone.)<br>・<b>Contrast</b>: 去年は 雨が 多かった<b>のに対して</b>、今年は 少ない。(Two things set side by side.)<br>A quick way to tell: <b>check whether a person or a whole clause comes before it</b>. After a person it is usually an attitude; after a plain form + の it is usually a contrast.<br>The distractors list the functions of other patterns, which have nothing to do with this one.',
  'n3-own-b2-19': 'Answer: <b>最中に</b>. Right in the middle of the meeting, the phone <b>happened</b> to ring. The core of this pattern is that sense of bad timing: <b>“interrupted just when things are in full swing.”</b><br>Distractors: <b>ところに</b> can also mean “just when…,” but its tone is more neutral; <b>うちに</b> is “while (you still can)…”; <b>たびに</b> is “every time….”<br>The hint is 「とつぜん」: <b>when the sentence has words like 「とつぜん」 or 「ちょうど」, or a feeling of “of all times,”</b> what comes before is usually 最中に.',
  'n3-own-b2-20': 'Answer: <b>して いる</b>. <b>A verb takes the te-iru form before 最中に</b>, because this pattern is about the moment something is <b>in progress</b>.<br>The ta-form, dictionary form and te-form distractors don\'t work.<br>Connections:<br>　・<b>verb te-iru form + 最中に</b> (話して いる最中に)<br>　・<b>noun + の + 最中に</b> (会議<u>の</u>最中に・食事<u>の</u>最中に)<br>The same sentence works both ways: 「食事を して いる最中に」 = 「食事の最中に」.',
  'n3-own-b2-21': 'Answer: <b>「最中に は じゃまされた 感じ、あいだに は ただの 時間の はんい」</b>.<br>・<b>最中に</b> = something <b>happens to</b> occur while things are in full swing, with a sense of bad timing, of being interrupted. (会議の 最中に 電話が 鳴った)<br>・<b>あいだに</b> = simply marks <b>a span of time</b>, with no feeling attached. (会議の あいだに 資料を 読んだ)<br>So if the second half is <b>something unexpected or disruptive</b>, use 最中に; if it is just <b>something done during that time</b>, use あいだに.<br>The distractor 「最中に は みじかい 時間、あいだに は 長い 時間」 is often taken to be the difference, but the two have <b>nothing to do with length of time</b>. The difference is whether there is a sense of being interrupted.',
  'n3-own-b2-22': 'Answer: <b>上に</b>. Tasty <b>and on top of that</b> cheap: two good points stacked in the same direction.<br>Distractors: <b>かわりに</b> is a trade-off (for “tasty but expensive” you would use it); <b>わりに</b> is “contrary to expectations” (安い わりに おいしい = tasty for the price); <b>に対して</b> is contrast.<br>Notice the <b>「も」</b> in the sentence: the も in 「値段<b>も</b> 安い」 signals adding on, and it goes together with 上に.',
  'n3-own-b2-23': 'Answer (the incorrect usage): <b>「この へやは 広い 上に、家賃が 高い。」</b>. <b>「広い」 is a plus and 「家賃が 高い」 is a minus</b>, so they point in opposite directions. <b>With 上に, both halves must be pluses or both must be minuses</b>.<br>What this sentence really means is “it\'s spacious, but the rent is high,” which calls for <b>〜が</b>／<b>〜けれど</b>／<b>〜かわりに</b>.<br>The other three all point the same way: 「広い 上に、駅からも 近い」 and 「頭が いい 上に、せいかくも いい」 each have two pluses, and 「道に まよった 上に、雨まで 降って きた」 has two misfortunes (getting lost, then rain).<br>Approach: <b>label each half “good” or “bad”; if one is good and the other bad, rule it out at once</b>.',
  'n3-own-b2-24': 'Answer: <b>な</b>. <b>A na-adjective keeps な before 上に</b>: しずか<u>な</u>上に.<br>Learn the connections as a set:<br>　・<b>verb / i-adjective plain form + 上に</b> (まよった上に／広い上に)<br>　・<b>na-adjective + な + 上に</b> (しずか<b>な</b>上に)<br>　・<b>noun + の + 上に</b> (雨<b>の</b>上に 風まで…)<br>These are exactly the same connections as わけがない (静か<b>な</b>わけがない／犯人<b>の</b>わけがない), so you can learn them together.<br>But <b>don\'t treat this as a universal rule</b>: in this same set, 「〜のに対して」 takes <b>な／である</b> after a noun, not の (学生<b>な</b>のに対して○／学生<b>の</b>のに対して✗). Check the connections one pattern at a time.',
  'n3-own-b2-25': 'Answer: <b>ものだ</b>. <b>Verb ta-form + ものだ</b> expresses <b>nostalgia for something you used to do often</b>: “I used to … a lot.”<br>Distractors: <b>ことだ</b> is advice to the listener; <b>ところだ</b> is “about to / in the middle of / just did”; <b>ばかりだ</b> is “have just done” or “keep on …ing (in one direction).”<br>Signature clue: <b>「子どもの ころは」「昔は」「学生時代は」 + 「よく」 + ta-form</b>. When this combination appears, the sentence almost always ends in ものだ.',
  'n3-own-b2-26': 'Answer: <b>ものだ</b>. This is its <b>other use: a general truth</b>, “as people grow older, they tend to recall the past.”<br>Distractors: <b>ことだ</b> is advice to a specific person; <b>しかない</b> is having no other choice; <b>とのことだ</b> is hearsay.<br>Learn the three uses of ものだ as a set:<br>　・<b>General truth</b>: 年を とると 昔を 思い出す<b>ものだ</b> (a universal tendency)<br>　・<b>Nostalgia</b>: よく 泳いだ<b>ものだ</b> (ta-form + ものだ)<br>　・<b>Deep feeling</b>: 時間が たつのは 早い<b>ものだ</b> (heartfelt reflection)<br>What the three share: <b>they talk not about one particular person but about a feeling that this is how things generally are</b>.',
  'n3-own-b2-27': 'Answer: <b>「ものだ は 一般的な 常識や 感慨、ことだ は 相手への ぐたいてきな アドバイス」</b>. Both follow verbs and look alike, but they work completely differently:<br>・<b>ものだ</b> = a general rule, social norm or feeling: <b>“this is how it is in general.”</b> It talks about <b>something true for anyone</b>.<br>　(人は だれでも まちがえる<b>ものだ</b>／目上の 人には 敬語を 使う<b>ものだ</b>)<br>・<b>ことだ</b> = concrete advice: <b>“this time, you\'d better do this.”</b> It targets <b>this matter for the person in front of you</b>.<br>　(かぜの ときは 早く 寝る<b>ことだ</b>)<br>How to tell them apart: <b>ask “is this stating a general rule, or writing a prescription for the person in front of me?”</b> Note that the normative use of ものだ (「約束は 守るものだ」) can also be said to someone\'s face, so you can\'t decide just by whether there is a listener. Look at whether it says <b>everyone should do this</b> or <b>you should do this now</b>.<br>The distractor swaps the two, which is the most typical misunderstanding.',
  'n3-own-b2-28': 'Answer: <b>ことです</b>. The doctor is <b>giving concrete advice</b> to the patient in front of them: “if you want to get better fast, go to bed early.”<br>Distractors: <b>ものです</b> is the one to be most careful with here. 「かぜの ときは 早く 寝るものです」 is valid and means <b>a general rule that you should go to bed early</b>, true for anyone. But this sentence has 「早く なおしたければ」, which pins it to this one patient, so what\'s needed is <b>a prescription for him</b>, not common sense for everyone. <b>ところです</b> is about a point in time; <b>ばかりです</b> is “just ….”<br>There are two hints: the parenthetical <b>「医者が 患者に」</b> names who is being addressed, and <b>「〜たければ」 + ことだ</b> is a very fixed pair, so the first half lets you predict the second.',
  'n3-own-b2-29': 'Answer: <b>しない</b>. <b>ことだ can follow either the dictionary form or the nai-form</b>; the nai-form advises someone not to do something.<br>The ta-form, te-form and masu-stem distractors don\'t work.<br>Connections:<br>　・<b>verb dictionary form + ことだ</b> = you\'d better do it (早く 寝る<b>ことだ</b>)<br>　・<b>verb nai-form + ことだ</b> = you\'d better not (むりを し<b>ない</b>ことだ)<br>Also note the 「〜ないように」 at the start of the sentence. That is another grammar point in this set (a hoped-for goal), so this sentence happens to link the two patterns.',
  'n3-own-b2-30': 'Answer: <b>相手への アドバイス</b>. The first half uses <b>「〜たければ」</b> (if you want to…), which already points to a listener, so the second half is advice to that person.<br>The distractors match other patterns:<br>　・Your own resolution → <b>〜ことにする</b> (毎日 続ける<b>ことにする</b>)<br>　・Something you heard → <b>〜とのことだ</b><br>　・A memory of the past → <b>ta-form + ものだ</b><br><b>「〜たいなら／〜たければ + 〜ことだ」 is a very fixed pair</b>, so the first half lets you predict the second.',
  'n3-own-b3-01': 'Answer: <b>かいけつ</b>.<br>Each distractor matches a typical mistake:<br>・<b>がいけつ</b>: confuses it with the sound of 「外（ガイ）」 or 「害（ガイ）」. <b>Note that this is not rendaku (sequential voicing)</b>: rendaku voices the first sound of the <b>second</b> part of a compound (that would be かい<b>げ</b>つ), so か→が on the first mora is something else.<br>・<b>かいけち</b>: some on\'yomi come in <b>two versions, ending in ツ or チ</b> (一: いち／いつ, 日: にち／じつ), and this option uses the wrong ending.<br>・<b>げけつ</b>: uses the other on\'yomi of 「解」, 「ゲ」 (解毒 = げどく), but in 「解決」 it is read 「カイ」.<br>It is normal for one kanji to have two on\'yomi, so <b>learn the reading together with the word; you can\'t work it out from the character alone</b>.',
  'n3-own-b3-02': 'Answer: <b>解決</b>. <b>「問題を 解決する」 is a fixed collocation</b>: the verb that goes with 問題 is 解決.<br>Distractors: <b>発展</b> is development (of an economy or a city); <b>意識</b> is being aware of something; <b>期待</b> is expectation. All three are frequent N3 kango (Sino-Japanese words), but none of them goes with 「問題」.<br>For words like these, the test point is usually not the reading but the <b>fixed collocation</b>, and learning them in pairs works better than learning single words:<br>　<b>問題を 解決する</b>／<b>期待に 応える</b>／<b>経済が 発展する</b>／<b>危険を 意識する</b>',
  'n3-own-b3-03': 'Answer: <b>「大きく のびて、進んで いく こと」</b>. 発展 = growing in scale or degree and moving forward (経済が 発展する・町が 発展する).<br>The distractor 「もとの 形に もどる こと」 is 「回復」 (recovery), 「小さく 分ける こと」 is 「分割」 (division), and 「前と 変わらない こと」 is 「相変わらず」 (as always). All of them are the opposite of 発展 or unrelated to it.<br>While we\'re here, let\'s separate two near-synonyms that are easy to mix up:<br>　・<b>発展</b> = moving toward something <b>bigger and broader</b> (経済・都市・関係)<br>　・<b>発達</b> = growing toward <b>maturity</b> (子どもの 発達・技術の 発達)<br>They are often interchangeable, but for a child growing up only 発達 works, and for a city becoming prosperous 発展 is more common.',
  'n3-own-b3-04': 'Answer: <b>ストレス</b>. 「ストレスが たまる」 is a fixed collocation: stress builds up.<br>Distractors: <b>チャンス</b> (chance), <b>アイデア</b> (idea) and <b>デザイン</b> (design) don\'t go with 「たまる」, and none of them keeps you awake at night.<br>Learn the common collocations of these loanwords together:<br>　<b>ストレスが たまる／ストレスを 感じる</b>・<b>チャンスを のがす／つかむ</b>・<b>アイデアが うかぶ</b>・<b>スケジュールが つまる</b>',
  'n3-own-b3-05': 'Answer: <b>スケジュール</b>.<br>All the distractors get the <b>long vowel</b> wrong, each in a different way: <b>スケジュル</b> drops it; <b>スケージュル</b> puts it in the wrong place; <b>スケジュウル</b> has it in the right place but writes it with う. Each one can be ruled out by its own test:<br>　① <b>Mora count</b>: スケジュル has only four morae (ス・ケ・ジュ・ル), while the other three have five. Counting morae only eliminates this one.<br>　② <b>Spelling rule</b>: in loanwords, a long vowel is <b>as a rule always written with 「ー」</b>, not with a vowel letter → スケジュウル is out.<br>　③ <b>Where the long vowel falls</b>: the sche- in schedule has a <b>short vowel</b>, and short vowels are not lengthened in Japanese. The long one is the /uː/ in -dule (British /ˈʃedjuːl/ and American /ˈskedʒuːl/ both have it) → ジュ<b>ー</b>ル, not ケー.<br>One caveat: <b>ー does not only come from a long vowel in the original word</b>. The final ー in サッカー (soccer) and コンピューター (computer) comes from -er, and neither ー in コーヒー (from Dutch koffie) is a long vowel in the original. <b>There is no rule you can work backward from, so in the end, learn the position of the long vowel with the whole word</b>.<br>Points on loanwords are lost almost entirely on <b>long vowels (ー) and small っ (sokuon)</b>: スケジュ<b>ー</b>ル, コ<b>ー</b>ヒ<b>ー</b>, サ<b>ッ</b>カー, チケ<b>ッ</b>ト.',
  'n3-own-b3-06': 'Answer: <b>アイデア</b>. 「アイデアが うかぶ」 = a good idea comes to mind.<br>Of the distractors, <b>チャンス</b> is the most tempting, because both いい チャンス and いい アイデア are natural phrases. What separates them is the verbs they take:<br>　・<b>アイデア</b> = a <b>method or plan</b> you come up with in your head (うかぶ・出す)<br>　・<b>チャンス</b> = an <b>opportunity</b> that turns up from outside (つかむ・のがす・来る)<br>How to decide: <b>ask whether the thing is something you thought up or something you came across</b>. What you think up is アイデア; what you come across is チャンス.',
  'n3-own-b3-07': 'Answer: <b>思わず</b> = involuntarily, couldn\'t help it.<br>The ending <b>「〜てしまいました」</b> is the key clue: it signals that it was not on purpose, and it goes together with 思わず.<br>Distractors: <b>さっそく</b> (right away, while you\'re keen) goes with deliberate actions; <b>相変わらず</b> is “as always”; <b>ますます</b> is “more and more.”<br>A general approach for this type of question: <b>look at the sentence ending first</b>. 〜てしまう → 思わず; 〜た (something achieved) → ついに; 〜ていく／〜てくる (gradual change) → ますます.',
  'n3-own-b3-08': 'Answer: <b>ついに</b> = finally. The long setup <b>「三年 かかって」</b> comes first, followed by an achievement: this is the typical structure for ついに.<br>Distractors: <b>思わず</b> is involuntarily; <b>ぜひ</b> goes with requests or wishes (ぜひ 来て ください); <b>まるで</b> goes with comparisons (まるで 夢の ようだ).<br>The near-synonym <b>やっと</b> also works here. The difference in nuance: <b>ついに is more objective and written, while やっと carries a sense of relief, “at long last.”</b>',
  'n3-own-b3-09': 'Answer: <b>相変わらず</b> = as always, the same as ever.<br>The hint is <b>「十年ぶりに」</b>: meeting again after a long time and finding him unchanged is exactly the contrast 相変わらず is used for.<br>Distractors: <b>さっそく</b> is right away; <b>ついに</b> is finally; <b>思わず</b> is involuntarily.<br>While you\'re at it, learn the typical context for <b>さっそく</b>: <b>doing something “right away” after getting something or hearing news</b>, as in 「本を 買って、さっそく 読み始めた」. It stresses not speed but <b>not putting it off, acting while you\'re keen</b>.',
  'n3-own-b3-10': 'Answer: <b>すっきり</b> = refreshed, clear. Feeling fresh after a shower is the most typical use of this word.<br>Distractors: <b>うっかり</b> is a moment of carelessness; <b>ふらふら</b> is dizzy and unsteady; <b>そっくり</b> is looking exactly alike.<br>すっきり is not only about the body: <b>a mind, a mood, a room or a piece of writing can all be すっきり</b>:<br>　・部屋を かたづけて すっきりした (the room is neat now)<br>　・説明を 聞いて すっきりした (my doubts cleared up)',
  'n3-own-b3-11': 'Answer: <b>ぴったり</b> = just right, a perfect fit.<br>Distractors: <b>うっかり</b> is careless; <b>すっきり</b> is refreshed; <b>はっきり</b> is clear, distinct.<br>Three common uses of ぴったり:<br>　・<b>Exact size</b>: サイズが ぴったり<br>　・<b>Exact time</b>: 8時ぴったりに 着いた<br>　・<b>A perfect match</b>: この 色は あなたに ぴったりだ<br>The shared core is <b>“no more, no less, exactly matching.”</b>',
  'n3-own-b3-12': 'Answer: <b>うっかり</b> = carelessly, by mistake. The ending <b>「〜てしまった」</b> shows it was not intentional and is a little regretted, which is exactly the nuance of うっかり.<br>Distractors: <b>ぴったり</b> is just right; <b>すっきり</b> is refreshed; <b>ゆっくり</b> is slowly.<br>Verbs that often go with うっかり are <b>忘れる・間違える・言う</b>, all things that shouldn\'t have happened and only did because of a lapse of attention:<br>　・うっかり 名前を 間違えた<br>　・うっかり しゃべって しまった',
  'n3-own-b3-13': 'Answer: <b>「ページを めくる 感覚と、背表紙を ながめる 楽しみ」</b>. What a demonstrative refers to is <b>almost always before it, and close by</b>. Right before 「それ」 are the two things introduced by the dash.<br>The other three options do appear in the passage, but they are far from 「それ」, and <b>they are all advantages of e-books</b>. Substituted in, the sentence would say “the advantages of e-books are something e-books don\'t have,” which contradicts itself.<br>How to solve it: <b>① look right before the demonstrative ② put each candidate into the sentence and read it through to see if it makes sense</b>. Step two filters out most distractors that appear in the text but are wrong.',
  'n3-own-b3-14': 'Answer: <b>「電子書籍が 増えても、紙の 本には 別の よさが ある」</b>. <b>What follows 「しかし」 is what the writer really wants to say</b>. The first half, on the advantages of e-books, is setup; the claim comes after the turn: paper books won\'t disappear, and they offer pleasures that e-books don\'t.<br>The distractor 「電子書籍は 便利なので、これからも 増えて いく」 only picks up what comes <b>before</b> the turn, which is the most common mistake. 「紙の 本は もう すぐ なくなって しまう」 contradicts the passage (it says 「なくなるわけではない」 = they won\'t disappear). 「本は 何百冊も 持ち歩く べきだ」 is not claimed anywhere in the passage.<br>A general rule for N3 short passages: <b>when you see 「しかし・ただし・でも」, underline it, because the main point comes after it.</b>',
  'n3-own-b3-15': 'Answer: <b>「紙の 本が ぜんぶ なくなる という ことでは ない」</b>. <b>「〜わけではない」 is a partial negation</b>: not entirely so, leaving some room. Here it means “it\'s not that paper books will all disappear” (e-books really are increasing, they just won\'t replace everything).<br>The distractor 「紙の 本が なくなる わけが ない」 uses <b>わけがない</b> (there is no way), which is much stronger and removes the room the writer left. <b>These two differ by only one particle (が ↔ では), and they are the most confused pair at N3</b>.<br>The distractors 「紙の 本は ぜったいに なくなる」 and 「紙の 本は もう なくなった」 read the negation as an affirmation.<br>Learn them as a pair: <b>わけがない = impossible</b> ／ <b>わけではない = not entirely</b>.',
  'n3-own-b3-16': 'Answer: <b>B 洋食コース</b>. Two conditions must both be met:<br>① <b>土曜日</b> (Saturday) → only A and B remain (C and D are on Sunday)<br>② <b>材料費こみで 2,300円 以内</b> (2,300円 or less including materials) → A: 2,000 + 500 = <b>2,500円</b> (over) ／ B: 1,800 + 500 = <b>2,300円</b> (exactly)<br>So only B works.<br><b>The key to this question is the first ※</b>: looking only at the prices in the table, A (2,000円) and B (1,800円) are both within 2,300, so you can\'t tell them apart. <b>Only after adding the 500円 materials fee charged every time do you get a single answer</b>.<br>In information-retrieval questions, always read every ※ note before you start comparing. Words in the notes such as 「毎回」, 「〜だけ」 and 「別途」 are often the only thing that separates the answers.',
  'n3-own-b3-17': 'Answer: <b>C 中華コース</b>. Filter by the three conditions one at a time:<br>① <b>駐車場代を はらいたくない</b> (no parking fees) → second ※: parking is free only on <b>Sunday</b> → C and D remain<br>② <b>午前中</b> (morning) → C is 10:00〜12:00 (morning), D is 14:00〜16:00 (afternoon) → only C remains<br>Distractor A is in the morning but on Saturday (parking costs money); B and D are both in the afternoon.<br><b>When there are many conditions, start with the one that eliminates the most options</b>. Here, “Sunday” cuts out half the options in one go, which is faster than looking at the times first.',
  'n3-own-b3-18': 'Answer: <b>「料金の ほかに 毎回 500円 はらう ひつようが ある」</b>. The first ※ says 「<b>毎回</b> かかります」: you pay 500円 every time, not just once.<br>The distractor 「材料費は 一回だけ はらえば いい」 misses 「毎回」. The other two, 「駐車場は いつでも 無料」 and 「土曜日も 無料に なる」, directly contradict the second ※, <b>「日曜日だけ」 (free only on Sunday)</b>.<br>The words most often used to set traps in ※ notes are these: <b>「毎回」「〜だけ」「〜のみ」「ただし」「別途」</b>. They are small, but they are often the only thing that separates the right answer. <b>Read the ※ notes first and the table second, not the other way around</b>.',
  'n3-own-b3-19': 'Answer: <b>ために</b>. This is the <b>purpose</b> use: saving money to buy a house.<br>How do you know it\'s not cause? <b>Look at the relationship between the two halves</b>: the saving is done precisely <b>to make buying the house happen</b>, so it\'s purpose. (The opposite case: in 「事故の ために 電車が 止まった」 the train did not stop to make the accident happen; the accident came first and the train stopped as a result, so that is cause.)<br>Distractors: <b>ように</b> also expresses purpose, but it follows a potential or negative form (買える<b>ように</b>), not the dictionary form of a volitional verb; <b>とおりに</b> is “just as…”; <b>おかげで</b> is cause with gratitude.<br>Substitution test: if 「〜を 目指して」 keeps the meaning, it\'s purpose; if 「〜が 原因で」 keeps the meaning, it\'s cause.<br>(Note that <b>you can\'t decide just by whether the second half is a volitional action</b>: the 「休んだ」 in 「病気の ために 会社を 休んだ」 is also something you chose to do, but that is cause.)',
  'n3-own-b3-20': 'Answer: <b>の</b>. <b>When a noun takes ために, use の</b>: 健康<u>の</u>ために.<br>Connections:<br>　・<b>verb dictionary form + ために</b> (家を 買うために)<br>　・<b>noun + の + ために</b> (健康<u>の</u>ために・子ども<u>の</u>ために)<br>This is the same rule as 「〜の間」, 「〜のせいで」 and 「〜の恐れがある」: <b>a noun takes の before a noun-like pattern</b>, so you don\'t need to learn it again for each pattern.<br>By the way, <b>the cause use connects in exactly the same way</b> (事故<u>の</u>ために), so the connection alone can\'t tell you whether it\'s purpose or cause. 「健康の ために 走る」 here is <b>purpose</b>: the running is done precisely to achieve 「健康」. The test is this relationship, not what kind of word comes first.',
  'n3-own-b3-21': 'Answer: <b>ように</b>. What comes before is the <b>potential form</b> 「話<u>せる</u>」, and <b>a potential form is not a volitional action you control</b>, so you need ように, not ために.<br><b>This is the core rule that divides ために and ように</b>:<br>　・<b>ために</b> + dictionary form of a volitional verb (家を <b>買う</b>ために): I can decide whether to do it<br>　・<b>ように</b> + potential form / negative form / non-volitional verb (<b>話せる</b>ように・<b>忘れない</b>ように・<b>直る</b>ように): I can\'t directly decide whether it happens<br>Compare:<br>　・日本語を <b>勉強する ために</b>、日本へ 行く (○ volitional)<br>　・日本語が <b>話せる ように</b>、毎日 練習する (○ potential form)',
  'n3-own-b3-22': 'Answer: <b>たって</b>. Replace the た of 「走った」 with <b>たって</b> = 走っ<b>たって</b>, a strong casual concessive (even if…).<br>Distractors: <b>たら</b> is conditional (走ったら = if you run), which doesn\'t go with 「いくら」 at the start; <b>たり</b> lists actions; <b>ては</b> doesn\'t form a sentence here.<br>The hints are <b>「いくら」</b> and the ending 「〜よ」: <b>いくら〜たって</b> is a fixed combination, and 「〜よ」 shows this is casual speech.<br>(Note: <b>「いくら 走っても」</b> means the same and is perfectly correct. ても is the general form and たって is its casual version. Because they are interchangeable, they never appear together among one question\'s options; another question on this point tests that they are equivalent.)',
  'n3-own-b3-23': 'Answer: <b>言ったって</b>. The method is simple: <b>change to the ta-form, then replace た with たって</b>.<br>　言う → 言っ<b>た</b> → 言っ<b>たって</b><br>The distractors attach to the dictionary form, add it to the te-form, or attach to the masu-stem, and none of them works.<br>The same rule applies to other verbs:<br>　・飲む → 飲んだ → <b>飲んだって</b><br>　・する → した → <b>したって</b><br>　・来る → 来た → <b>来たって</b><br><b>If you can make the ta-form, you can make たって</b>; there is no separate rule to memorize.',
  'n3-own-b3-24': 'Answer: <b>「今から 行っても、店は もう 閉まって いる」</b>. <b>〜たって = 〜ても</b>: the meaning is exactly the same, and the only difference is that <b>たって is casual and stronger in tone</b>.<br>The distractor 「今から 行けば、店は まだ 開いて いる」 reads the concessive as a condition; 「今から 行った ほうが いい」 is advice; 「今から 行く ことに した」 is a decision.<br>Choosing a register:<br>　・<b>〜ても</b>: general, fine in writing too<br>　・<b>〜たって</b>: casual, often with a feeling of “it\'s no use”<br>Use ても in essays and formal letters; you\'ll only see たって in conversation between friends or in dialogue in novels.',
  'n3-own-b3-25': 'Answer: <b>ように</b>. What comes before is the <b>negative form</b> 「忘れ<u>ない</u>」, and <b>ように follows potential forms, negative forms and non-volitional verbs</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>ために</b> needs the dictionary form of a volitional verb and cannot follow a nai-form; <b>ところに</b> is a point in time; <b>ばかりに</b> is “just because… (things ended up badly).”<br>Learn the three most common shapes of this pattern as a set:<br>　・<b>〜ないように</b> (忘れないように・遅れないように)<br>　・<b>potential form + ように</b> (読めるように・間に合うように)<br>　・<b>non-volitional verb + ように</b> (風邪が 治るように)',
  'n3-own-b3-26': 'Answer: <b>「子どもにも 読める（　　）、ふりがなを つけた。」</b>. 「読<b>める</b>」 is the <b>potential form</b>, a state you can\'t directly decide, so you need <b>ように</b>: 子どもにも 読める<b>ように</b>、ふりがなを つけた.<br>The other three should all use <b>ために</b>:<br>　・「留学する」: dictionary form of a volitional verb<br>　・「大学に 入る」: dictionary form of a volitional verb<br>　・「家族の」: noun + の<br>Only one thing decides it: <b>is the verb before the blank an action you control?</b><br>　・Yes (買う・勉強する・留学する) → <b>ために</b><br>　・No (買える・忘れない・治る) → <b>ように</b>',
  'n3-own-b3-27': 'Answer: <b>ように</b>. This is another use of ように: <b>ending the sentence with ように to express a wish or prayer</b> (I hope…).<br>Distractors: <b>ために</b> can\'t end a sentence on its own; <b>ことに</b> and <b>そうに</b> don\'t form a sentence here.<br>The fixed form for wishes is <b>「どうか／どうぞ 〜ますように」</b>, common on ema (wooden prayer plaques) at shrines, in birthday wishes and in farewell messages:<br>　・どうか 元気で いられますように<br>　・みなさんが しあわせに なりますように<br>Note that this use <b>has no second half</b>: the sentence ends at ように.',
  'n3-own-b3-28': 'Answer: <b>ことに なりました</b>. The parenthetical makes clear it is <b>the company\'s decision</b>: not your own choice but an arranged outcome, so use <b>ことになる</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>ことに しました</b> is your own decision; <b>ものです</b> is common sense or a feeling; <b>とのことです</b> reports what someone else said, but the subject is 「私」, and you wouldn\'t use a hearsay form for your own transfer.<br>Learn them as a pair:<br>　・<b>〜ことにする</b> = <b>I decide</b> (personal will)<br>　・<b>〜ことになる</b> = <b>it has been decided / it turned out this way</b> (objective outcome)<br>Japanese speakers often use ことになる to <b>play down their own role</b>: even when they chose something themselves, they say 「〜ことになりました」, which sounds less self-assertive.',
  'n3-own-b3-29': 'Answer: <b>ことに しました</b>. Quitting smoking is <b>your own resolution</b>, so use ことにする.<br>Distractors: <b>ことに なりました</b> would mean “(by some rule) I have to stop smoking,” which puts the responsibility somewhere else entirely; <b>ように なりました</b> is a natural change in ability or state (吸えるように なった = became able to smoke); <b>ものです</b> is common sense or a feeling.<br>How these three patterns divide the work:<br>　・<b>ことにする</b> = I decide (will)<br>　・<b>ことになる</b> = it gets decided (arrangement)<br>　・<b>ようになる</b> = come to be able to (change in ability)',
  'n3-own-b3-30': 'Answer: <b>「自分で 決めて、しゅうかんに して いる」</b>. <b>「〜ことにしている」 is the continuing form of ことにする</b>: you decided it yourself and <b>keep it up as a habit</b>.<br>Distractors:<br>　・「会社の きそくで 走らされて いる」 is a rule imposed on you → <b>ことになっている</b> (会社では 8時に 出社する<b>ことになっている</b>)<br>　・「走れるように なった」 is a change in ability → <b>ようになった</b><br>　・「走った ことが ある」 is experience → <b>たことがある</b><br>Learn these two 「〜ている」 forms as a pair:<br>　・<b>〜ことにしている</b> = a habit I set for myself<br>　・<b>〜ことになっている</b> = a rule or custom (not set by me)',
  'n4-own-b1-01': 'Answer: <b>かたく</b> (硬い, hard). Bread left out too long goes hard. All four options are the く-form of an i-adjective, so only the context can decide.<br>Distractors: <b>あまく</b> (sweet) has nothing to do with being old; <b>つめたく</b> (cold) describes temperature, not texture; <b>ひろく</b> (wide) is for space and cannot describe bread.<br>In this question type every option has a correct form. What is tested is purely how the word\'s meaning fits the context.',
  'n4-own-b1-02': 'Answer: <b>美しかった</b>. For the beauty of scenery, Japanese typically uses words like <b>美しい・きれい・すばらしい</b>; of the four options, only <b>美しい</b> belongs to this group.<br>Distractors: <b>楽しい</b> describes an activity that makes people happy, and scenery itself is not 楽しい; <b>うるさい</b> mainly means noisy to the ear (by extension it can mean too busy in color), and it does not fit a view seen from a mountain; <b>優しい</b> describes a person\'s character.<br>Here <b>all four options are correctly formed</b> (all are past forms of i-adjectives). The question tests only <b>which word goes with this noun</b>, so ask about collocation, not conjugation.',
  'n4-own-b1-03': 'Answer: <b>めずらしい</b> (珍しい) = rare. It is rare, so you seldom see it: the two halves are cause and effect.<br>Distractors: <b>うつくしい</b> (beautiful): being pretty does not stop you from seeing it; <b>たのしい</b> (fun) and <b>やさしい</b> (kind) cannot describe how rare a flower is.<br>All four are i-adjectives with correct forms, so <b>you cannot eliminate by form; only meaning and the cause-effect link decide</b>. This is the difference between N4 and N5 vocabulary questions: N5 often tests conjugated forms, while N4 often asks which of four correctly formed words makes sense.',
  'n4-own-b1-04': 'Answer: <b>音（おと）</b>. A piano is an object, and the sound an object makes is 音.<br>Distractors: <b>声（こえ）</b> is only for sounds a person or animal makes with its voice. This is a classic trap: English happily says “the sound of a piano” and “the sound of a voice” with one word, but Japanese splits them strictly. <b>味</b> is what you taste and <b>におい</b> is what you smell; neither works with 聞こえる.<br>Remember the pair: <b>音 = objects, 声 = people or animals</b>.',
  'n4-own-b1-05': 'Answer: <b>におい</b>. Something drifting from the kitchen that you notice with your nose is におい.<br>Distractors: <b>味（あじ）</b> is only what you taste once the food is in your mouth. The speaker is outside the kitchen and has not eaten yet, so 味 does not work. <b>音</b> is sound and <b>色</b> is visual.<br>Fixed expressions: <b>においが する</b> / <b>味が する</b>. Both use する, not あります.',
  'n4-own-b1-06': 'Answer: <b>声（こえ）</b>. A baby is a person, and any sound made with the vocal cords is 声.<br>Distractor: <b>音</b> is for objects, so 泣く音 does not work in Japanese. This question is deliberately paired with the previous one (ピアノの音): in both you hear a sound, and whether the source is an object or a living thing decides the word.<br>Once more: <b>雨の音・車の音</b> / <b>人の声・犬の声</b>.',
  'n4-own-b1-07': 'Answer: <b>心配</b>. 心配を かける = to make someone worry; a fixed collocation.<br>Distractors: <b>経験</b> (experience) does not take かける; <b>失礼</b> means rudeness and is used as 失礼します / 失礼な人; <b>お辞儀</b> is the act of bowing and takes する.<br>A matching collocation: <b>心配を かける</b> (cause someone worry) / <b>迷惑を かける</b> (cause someone trouble). Both use かける, so learn them together.',
  'n4-own-b1-08': 'Answer: <b>お辞儀</b>. The act of bowing when you meet someone is お辞儀, used with する.<br>Distractors: <b>心配</b> goes with かける and does not fit this context; <b>失礼</b> means the opposite, since bowing on meeting is not rude; <b>経験</b> makes no sense here.<br>What these words share is that <b>most of them can take する to become verbs</b> (心配する・経験する・お辞儀する), but each has its own particles and contexts, so learn them as a set.',
  'n4-own-b1-09': 'Answer: <b>失礼</b>. Leaving without a greeting is rude behavior; 失礼な ことを する is a fixed expression.<br>The three distractors <b>are also na-adjectives</b>, so form cannot rule them out; only meaning can: <b>心配な ことを した</b> (did something worrying) does not fit, because the point here is manners, not risk; <b>安心な</b> (reassuring) means the opposite; <b>熱心な</b> (eager) describes an enthusiastic attitude and cannot describe rude behavior.<br>Also note: <b>失礼します</b> (said when entering or leaving a room, or ending a phone call) and <b>失礼な人</b> (a rude person) are two common uses of the same word.',
  'n4-own-b1-10': 'Answer: <b>思い出</b>. <b>いい思い出になる is a fixed expression</b>: a past experience stays with you and becomes a good memory.<br>Distractors: <b>夢</b> means a hope for the future or a dream while sleeping, and cannot になる into something in the past; <b>景色</b> is scenery in front of you, not something kept in your heart; <b>世話</b> means care, used as 世話を する / 世話に なる, so いい世話になる does not work.<br>In one line: <b>思い出 = already happened, kept in your heart</b>; <b>夢 = not yet happened, something you want to happen</b>.',
  'n4-own-b1-11': 'Answer: <b>夢</b>. 〜たいという + noun wraps a wish into a noun phrase, so the noun after it must express a wish.<br>Distractors: <b>思い出</b> is a past memory and cannot follow a future wish like 〜たい; <b>世話</b> and <b>景色</b> make no sense here.<br>The pattern itself is also tested: <b>[plain form] + という + noun</b> turns a whole clause into the content of a noun (医者に なりたい<u>という</u>夢).',
  'n4-own-b1-12': 'Answer: <b>世話</b>. 世話を する = to take care of someone.<br>Distractors: <b>経験</b> cannot express caring for someone with 〜をしてくれる; <b>思い出</b> and <b>夢</b> make no sense here.<br>Learn the three forms as a set: <b>世話を する</b> (I take care of someone) / <b>世話に なる</b> (I am cared for by someone) / <b>お世話に なりました</b> (thank you for taking care of me, a set phrase of thanks). The direction changes, and so does the particle.',
  'n4-own-b1-13': 'Answer: <b>中止</b>. When an event is called off, use 中止に なる.<br>Distractors: <b>留守</b> means not at home and is used for people; <b>自由</b> means freedom; <b>嘘</b> means a lie. None relates to canceling an event.<br>The shared pattern is the real point: <b>noun + に なりました</b> shows a change of state, and these words (中止・自由・留守) often appear in it.',
  'n4-own-b1-14': 'Answer: <b>留守</b>. Nobody answers after several calls, so the person is not home: 留守.<br>Distractors: <b>中止</b> is for events, not people; <b>自由</b> and <b>失礼</b> make no sense.<br>〜のようです is a guess based on evidence in front of you (no answer → probably not home). These state nouns often pair with <b>のようです</b> (guess) or <b>に なりました</b> (change), so when you see either form, think of this group first.',
  'n4-own-b1-15': 'Answer: <b>自由</b>. 自由に 使えます = you can use it freely. 自由 is a na-adjective, and it becomes 自由<u>に</u> when it modifies a verb.<br>Distractors: <b>中止</b>, <b>留守</b> and <b>嘘</b> cannot take に 使えます.<br>Also note だれでも in the first half: <b>question word + でも</b> means “anyone at all,” another N4 point.',
  'n4-own-b1-16': 'Answer: <b>ぜひ</b>. <b>ぜひ goes with request or wish endings</b> (〜てください・〜たい・〜ましょう・〜てほしい) and expresses a strong recommendation or wish.<br>Distractors: <b>なかなか</b> in the sense of “just won\'t” needs a negative (なかなか 来ません), so it does not fit てください; <b>やっと</b> means “finally” and is used when something has come about; <b>あまり</b> in the sense of “not very” also needs a negative.<br>(Their affirmative uses <b>take different forms, so keep them apart</b>: <b>なかなか</b> can go straight with an affirmative (なかなか いい = quite good); <b>あまり</b> must become <b>あまりに（も）</b> to mean “too” (あまりに 高い = too expensive). Plain あまり still only goes with negatives.)<br>The method for this type: <b>look at the sentence ending first</b>. If it is てください, pick ぜひ; if it is negative, pick なかなか / あまり.',
  'n4-own-b1-17': 'Answer: <b>なかなか</b>. <b>When なかなか means “just won\'t,” a negative follows</b>, and the ending 来ません is the key.<br>Distractors: <b>ぜひ</b> goes with てください / たいです; <b>きっと</b> goes with a guess (きっと 来るでしょう); <b>だんだん</b> goes with gradual change, not a simple negative.<br>Learn as a pair: <b>なかなか〜ない</b> (just won\'t...) / <b>やっと〜た</b> (finally did...). One has not happened yet; the other has finally happened.',
  'n4-own-b1-18': 'Answer: <b>だんだん</b>. <b>だんだん goes with gradual change like 〜くなってきました</b>: it gets warmer day by day.<br>Distractors: <b>もうすぐ</b> (soon) is about something <b>that has not happened yet</b>, which clashes with なって きました, a change already under way; <b>ぜひ</b> goes with requests or wishes (ぜひ 来て ください); <b>なかなか</b> carries either “just won\'t” (with a negative) or an evaluative “more than expected,” and is not used for the simple, steady change of seasons.<br>For this whole set of adverbs, the clue is <b>the form of the sentence ending</b>: てください → ぜひ, negative → なかなか, 〜た (achieved) → やっと, 〜てきました (gradual change) → だんだん, 〜でしょう (guess) → きっと.',
  'n4-own-b1-19': 'Answer: <b>見学</b>. Visiting a place <b>in order to learn</b> (a factory, school or institution) is 見学.<br>Distractors: <b>見物</b> also means visiting, but for <b>sightseeing</b> (お祭りを 見物する), not for learning at a factory. This is the most confusing pair in the set. <b>利用</b> means use; <b>生産</b> means production, and a factory produces cars, not the factory itself.<br>In one line: <b>見学 = go to learn</b>, <b>見物 = go to watch the show</b>.',
  'n4-own-b1-20': 'Answer: <b>工事</b>. A common reason for a traffic jam is roadwork: 工事を する / 工事中.<br>Distractors: <b>見学</b> is a study visit; <b>説明</b> is an explanation; <b>準備</b> is preparation. None of them would jam a road.<br>For this group of Sino-Japanese nouns that take する, the test is <b>picking the right noun for the context</b>; the する part itself is not difficult.',
  'n4-own-b1-21': 'Answer: <b>説明</b>. You do not know how to use it, so you ask the other person to explain.<br>Distractors: <b>生産</b> is production; <b>見物</b> is sightseeing; <b>中止</b> is cancellation. None of them answers the need.<br>Note the ending <b>〜してください</b>: it is a request to the listener, so the blank needs <b>the action you want the listener to do</b>. First check who is asked to do what, and the options are easy to eliminate.',
  'n4-own-b1-22': 'Answer: <b>見つかりました</b>. The subject is かぎ<u>が</u>: the key itself “was found,” so you need <b>the intransitive verb 見つかる</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>見つけました</b> is transitive, and the sentence would have to be （私は）かぎ<u>を</u> 見つけました, so the particle does not match; <b>見えました</b> means “could be seen” (naturally), not “found”; <b>見ました</b> means “looked at.”<br>Learn as a pair: <b>Nが 見つかる</b> (intransitive) / <b>Nを 見つける</b> (transitive). <b>The particle, が or を, tells you which verb to use.</b>',
  'n4-own-b1-23': 'Answer: <b>さそい</b> (誘う). AをBに 誘う = invite A to B; a fixed pattern.<br>Distractors: <b>伝える</b> means to pass on (a message), so its object is words, not a person\'s action; <b>比べる</b> means to compare; <b>写す</b> means to copy or take a photo.<br>The pattern itself is the clue: with <b>人を + place/activity に</b>, the verb is almost always 誘う or 連れて行く.',
  'n4-own-b1-24': 'Answer: <b>くらべ</b> (比べる). AとBを 比べる / ふたつの〜を 比べる = compare the two.<br>Distractors: the object of <b>誘う</b> is a person; the object of <b>伝える</b> is a message; with <b>切る</b>, 写真を 切りました is a valid sentence on its own, but the opening どちらが きれいか 知りたくて shows the speaker wants <b>a judgment after comparing</b>, and cutting photos would not give that.<br>There are two clues: <b>ふたつの</b> and the opening <b>どちらが きれいか</b>. When a sentence has “two” plus “which one is more...,” the verb is almost always 比べる.<br>(Watch the forms: all four options are masu-stems, so all work with ました. If the stem used the te-form, さそい and きり would become the non-existent ×さそいて / ×きりて. Godan verbs take the sound change: 誘って, 切って.)',
  'n4-own-b1-25': 'Answer: <b>以外（いがい）</b> = except for. Works every day except Sunday.<br>Distractors: <b>意外（いがい）</b> is pronounced <b>exactly the same</b> but means unexpected; only the kanji and the context tell them apart. That is the core of this point. <b>以内</b> means within (a range, the opposite direction); <b>意見</b> means opinion.<br>How to tell: <b>以外</b> follows the thing being excluded (日曜日以外); <b>意外</b> is usually followed by に + adjective or な + noun.',
  'n4-own-b1-26': 'Answer: <b>意外（いがい）</b> = unexpectedly. You thought it would be hard, but it was easy.<br>Distractor: <b>以外</b> has the same reading but means except for, and 以外に かんたんでした makes no sense. This question is deliberately paired with the previous one: <b>same reading, different position and partners</b>.<br>Remember the patterns: <b>意外に + adjective</b> (意外に 簡単でした), <b>意外な + noun</b> (意外な 結果). <b>If に or な follows, it is 意外, not 以外.</b>',
  'n4-own-b1-27': 'Answer: <b>会い</b> (会う) = meet a person. 人<u>に</u> 会う takes the particle に.<br>Distractors: <b>合う</b> is also read あう but means to fit or match (サイズが 合う); it is the classic homophone of 会う. <b>開く（あく）</b> means to open and <b>空く（あく）</b> means to become empty; they are read あく, not あう, and look similar.<br>How to tell: <b>the object is a person → 会う</b>; <b>the object is a size, opinion or answer → 合う</b>.',
  'n4-own-b1-28': 'Answer: <b>はんたい</b>.<br>The biggest trap is <b>はんだい</b>. After learning rendaku in N5 (て + かみ → てがみ), it is easy to assume that the second half always becomes voiced when two words join. But <b>rendaku is not a general rule</b>: <b>on\'yomi compounds like 反対 usually do not undergo it</b>.<br>Other distractors: <b>ほんたい</b> reads 反 with the sound of 本; <b>ばんたい</b> voices the first half.<br>Rule of thumb: <b>native compounds read in kun\'yomi often take rendaku; Sino-Japanese compounds read in on\'yomi often do not.</b>',
  'n4-own-b1-29': 'Answer: <b>せかい</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>せがい</b> is a misreading caused by the rendaku instinct; 世界 is an on\'yomi compound and does <b>not</b> take rendaku. <b>よかい</b> wrongly uses the kun\'yomi よ of 世 (世の中 = よのなか); <b>せいかい</b> adds a long vowel, which is the reading of 正解.<br>Other on\'yomi compounds without rendaku: <b>意見（いけん）</b>, <b>説明（せつめい）</b>, <b>洗濯（せんたく）</b>.<br>(Note that 心配 cannot serve as an example: 配 alone is read はい and becomes ぱい here, which is the sound change after ん.)',
  'n4-own-b1-30': 'Answer: <b>あんしん</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>あんじん</b> comes from the rendaku instinct; <b>やすしん</b> and <b>あんこころ</b> <b>mix on\'yomi and kun\'yomi</b>, reading 安 with its kun\'yomi (やすい) or 心 with its kun\'yomi (こころ) and attaching it to the other half\'s on\'yomi.<br><b>Sino-Japanese compounds are, as a rule, read entirely in on\'yomi</b>, not half on\'yomi and half kun\'yomi. When an option mixes a kun half with an on half, you can usually eliminate it right away.',
  'n4-own-b2-01': 'Answer: <b>「きのう、じどうしゃの 工場を 見学しました。」</b>. 見学 = <b>visiting a place in order to learn</b>; its objects are places such as factories, schools and institutions.<br>The other three sentences <b>have no grammar errors at all</b>. What is wrong is whether the word fits the sentence:<br>・「デパートで くつを 見学して、買いました。」: looking at shoes to buy is shopping; use 見る.<br>・「弟の しゅくだいを 見学して あげました。」: use 見る.<br>・「週末は 家で テレビを 見学しました。」: watching TV is simply 見る.<br>The method for usage questions: <b>first ask what range of things the word applies to, then see which sentence falls inside it</b>. Do not just check whether the grammar works.',
  'n4-own-b2-02': 'Answer: <b>「テーブルに 花を かざりました。」</b>. 飾る = <b>to set something out so it looks nice</b>; its objects are decorations such as flowers, pictures and photos.<br>The other three sentences all describe <b>putting something away</b>, the opposite direction:<br>・「ふゆの ふくを たんすに かざりました。」: putting clothes in a dresser needs 入れる or しまう.<br>・「のこった ごはんを れいぞうこに かざりました。」: use 入れる.<br>・「読みおわった 本を かばんに かざりました。」: use 入れる.<br>The trap here is <b>mixing up “setting out” and “putting away.”</b> In English you can “put” flowers on a table and also “put” clothes in a dresser, but Japanese keeps the two apart.',
  'n4-own-b2-03': 'Answer: <b>「友だちの 家へ 行きましたが、るすでした。」</b>. 留守 = <b>a person is not at home</b>; the subject must be a person or a person\'s home.<br>The other three all go wrong by <b>using 留守 for something that is not a person</b>:<br>・「きょうは 学校が るすです。」: for a day off, use 休み.<br>・「かばんの 中が るすです。」: use 空（から）.<br>・「電車が るすだったので、すわれました。」: use 空（す）いて いた.<br>Once you pin down this word\'s range (<b>only for a person being out</b>), you can eliminate three options at once.',
  'n4-own-b2-04': 'Answer: <b>で</b>. Amount of time + で + verb shows <b>the length of time it took to finish something</b>: it was done in 20 minutes.<br>Distractors:<br>・<b>に</b> marks <b>the point in time when something happens</b> (3時に 始まります). Here the point is how long it took, not what time.<br>・<b>を</b> cannot follow a time quantity and then take an intransitive verb (20分を 終わりました does not work).<br>・<b>しか</b> <b>must be followed by a negative</b>, but the ending 終わりました is affirmative, a direct contradiction.<br>The key: <b>is the ending an affirmative statement of completion?</b>',
  'n4-own-b2-05': 'Answer: <b>で</b>. The reply means “it took 30 minutes to finish,” stressing the length of time spent.<br>Distractors: <b>に</b> marks a point in time; <b>から</b> marks a starting point (30分から = starting from minute 30, which makes no sense); <b>まで</b> marks an end point.<br>In N5 you learned that で marks a <b>place</b> (教室で), a <b>means</b> (バスで) and a <b>total</b> (全部で 千円). The N4 use for “time spent” extends the same で; it is not a new particle.',
  'n4-own-b2-06': 'Answer: <b>に</b>. This question is deliberately paired with the previous two: <b>with time, で and に mark completely different things</b>.<br>・<b>3時に 始まります</b> = starts at <b>the point</b> of three o\'clock.<br>・<b>20分で 終わりました</b> = finished in <b>a span</b> of twenty minutes.<br>Distractors: <b>で</b> would mean “using the span of three o\'clock,” which makes no sense; <b>を</b> cannot mark a point in time; <b>まで</b> marks an end point (3時まで = until three), which contradicts 始まります.<br>How to judge: <b>is the time before the blank a point or an amount?</b> A point takes に, an amount takes で.<br>(Note: 3時<b>から</b> 始まります is also perfectly correct Japanese and stresses the starting point, “from three o\'clock.” Because both it and に are right, they will not appear as options in the same question.)',
  'n4-own-b2-07': 'Answer: <b>泣かせる</b>. 泣く is a Group 1 (godan) verb. For the causative, <b>move the final sound back to the あ-row and add せる</b>: 泣<u>く</u> → 泣<u>か</u> → 泣<b>かせる</b>.<br>Each distractor matches one confusion:<br>・<b>泣かれる</b> is the <b>passive</b>; it changes in the same place but adds れる.<br>・<b>泣ける</b> is the <b>potential</b> (can cry); the final sound moves to the え-row and adds る.<br>・<b>泣かさせる</b> mixes Group 1 せる with Group 2 させる.<br>Learn the whole set: Group 1 → あ-row + <b>せる</b>, Group 2 → drop る + <b>させる</b>, する → <b>させる</b>, くる → <b>こさせる</b>.',
  'n4-own-b2-08': 'Answer: <b>を</b>. The basic causative pattern is <b>AをV-させる</b> = to make A feel or end up in some state. Here it means “made my parents worry,” and the parents are <b>the ones affected, who react</b>, so they take を.<br>Distractors: <b>に</b> is the real trap. Causees can take に (see the next question), but <b>a sentence like 心配させる, which makes someone feel an emotion, always uses を</b>; <b>が</b> would make the parents the subject, but the subject is 弟; <b>で</b> marks a place or means.<br>Note that this を is not a simple direct object: <b>it marks the person who is made to feel or end up a certain way</b>.',
  'n4-own-b2-09': 'Answer: <b>に</b>. <b>This question is the counterpart of the previous one; learn them as a pair.</b><br>飲む is transitive, so this causative verb already has a を (くすり<u>を</u>). The causee <b>must then take に</b>, so that <b>one verb</b> does not carry two を. 私を くすりを 飲ませた is not acceptable.<br>Distractors: <b>を</b> is exactly that error; <b>が</b> would make 私 the subject, but the subject is 母; <b>で</b> marks a means or place.<br>Three cases, decided by <b>whether this causative verb already has a を object</b> and <b>whether the action is something the causee controls</b>:<br>・<b>Already has a を object</b> (くすり<u>を</u> 飲ませる) → the causee can only take <b>に</b>, so one verb does not carry two を.<br>・<b>No を object, and the action is under the causee\'s control</b> (行かせる・遊ばせる・読ませる) → either を or に; <b>を leans toward forcing</b>, <b>に toward allowing</b> (if the sentence already has a destination に, the causee takes を: 子どもを 学校に 行かせる).<br>・<b>No を object, and it is an involuntary reaction</b> (泣かせる・驚かせる・困らせる・喜ばせる・心配させる) → <b>always を</b>.<br>The test only looks at <b>the same verb</b>: in the previous question, けがを して、両親を 心配させました does contain an earlier を, but it belongs to けがをする, not to 心配させる, so it does not count.',
  'n4-own-b2-10': 'Answer: <b>も</b>. Number + も shows <b>a quantity larger than expected</b>, with surprise or emphasis: he ate a whole eight.<br>Distractors: <b>に</b>, <b>が</b> and <b>で</b> cannot follow a number directly to mean “as many as.”<br>The も from N5 means <b>“also”</b> (私<b>も</b> 行きます). After <b>a number</b> <b>and with an affirmative</b>, it means something else: <b>“as many as.”</b><br>(With a negative the meaning flips to “not even”: 一時間も 寝て いません. See the next question.)',
  'n4-own-b2-11': 'Answer: <b>しか</b>. <b>しか is always followed by a negative</b> and means “only (so little)”: slept only three hours. The ending 寝られませんでした is the key.<br>Distractors: <b>だけ</b> also means “only,” but it <b>goes with an affirmative</b> (三時間だけ 寝ました○ / 三時間だけ 寝られませんでした✗); this is the contrast most worth remembering. <b>まで</b> marks an end point; <b>が</b> does not form a sentence here.<br>Learn as a set:<br>・<b>number + しか + negative</b> = only this little (三時間しか 寝られませんでした)<br>・<b>number + だけ + affirmative</b> = only this little (三時間だけ 寝ました)<br>・<b>number + も + affirmative</b> = as many as this (八つも 食べました)<br>(Note: number + も <b>can also take a negative</b>, but then it means “not even”: 一時間も 寝て いません. That is a separate use; do not mix it up with the ones above.)',
  'n4-own-b2-12': 'Answer: <b>「思って いたより 時間が かかって、おどろいて いる」</b>. も after a number <b>carries the judgment “more than expected”</b>; it is not just a neutral report of the figure.<br>Distractors:<br>・「時間が 少なくて うれしいと 思って いる」 is the opposite direction; that would need しか.<br>・「ほかの 人も 二時間 かかったと 言って いる」 treats it as the N5 も meaning “also.” It is the same particle, but after <b>a number</b> it means “as many as.”<br>・「二時間ぐらいだろうと よそうして いる」 is a guess, which would need ぐらいでしょう.<br>In one line: <b>when you see number + も, read the speaker\'s feeling that it is too much</b>.',
  'n4-own-b2-13': 'Answer: <b>作られる</b>. 作る is a Group 1 verb. For the passive, <b>change the final sound from the う-row to the あ-row and add れる</b>: 作<u>る</u> → 作<u>ら</u> → 作<b>られる</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>作らせる</b> is the <b>causative</b> (make someone make); <b>作れる</b> is the <b>potential</b> (can make); <b>作られさせる</b> stacks two changes together.<br>The causative and passive change <b>in exactly the same place</b> (both go back to the あ-row); the only difference is whether <b>せる</b> or <b>れる</b> follows. That one kana is the difference between two grammar points.',
  'n4-own-b2-14': 'Answer: <b>によって</b>. In a passive sentence it marks <b>who did the action</b>, in fairly formal contexts such as making, inventing or deciding.<br>Distractors:<br>・<b>から</b> marks <b>a starting point or the source material</b>, not the maker.<br>・<b>を</b> cannot mark the agent of a passive.<br>・<b>へ</b> marks direction.<br>Pattern: <b>Aによって（Bが）V-られる</b>. The real subject (the thing made, じしょ) takes は or が, and the agent takes によって.',
  'n4-own-b2-15': 'Answer: <b>から</b>. Here rice is <b>the raw material</b>; after processing you can no longer see its original form, so から is used.<br>Distractor: <b>によって</b> marks <b>the party who does the action, or the cause of an event</b> (外国人によって 作られた / 地震によって こわされた). Rice here is <b>the material being processed</b>, not the one acting, so it takes から. This question is deliberately paired with the previous one to make you tell these apart.<br>By the way, for a material whose <b>original form is still visible after processing</b> (木で 作った いす), <b>で</b> is the usual choice.<br>Summary: <b>a person → によって</b>, <b>raw material (transformed) → から</b>, <b>material (still visible) → で</b>.',
  'n4-own-b2-16': 'Answer: <b>だれでも</b>. Question word + でも means <b>“anyone at all,”</b> a universal affirmative.<br>Distractors: <b>だれが / だれに / だれを</b> are <b>question words with particles, used to ask questions</b>. But this sentence continues with が、よやくが ひつようです: it is <b>stating a rule</b>, not asking.<br>First step: <b>check whether the sentence is a statement or a question</b>. If it is a statement, think of question word + でも.',
  'n4-own-b2-17': 'Answer: <b>どこでも</b> = you can buy it anywhere.<br>Distractors: <b>どこが / どこに</b> are for asking; <b>どこか</b> means “somewhere” (one unspecified place), a completely different tone. 日本のどこかで買えます would mean “you can buy it somewhere in Japan,” suggesting it is hard to find, the opposite of the intended meaning.<br>The good news is that this pattern <b>works for the whole set</b>, so you need not memorize each one: <b>だれでも・何でも・いつでも・どこでも・どれでも</b>.',
  'n4-own-b2-18': 'Answer: <b>何でも</b> = take anything you like.<br>Distractors: <b>何が / 何を / 何に</b> are question-word combinations for asking, and they do not form a sentence before 〜てください.<br>Both word orders are natural (何でも 好きな ものを and 好きな ものを 何でも); the only difference is focus, not grammar.<br>The sentence <b>gives permission</b>; it is not asking what the listener wants. That reading of the context is what leads to the right answer.',
  'n4-own-b2-19': 'Answer: <b>なら</b>. The other person offered two choices (あした / あさって), and B <b>picks out the one that works</b>. This is the most typical use of なら.<br>Distractors: <b>だから</b> shows cause, but there is no cause-effect link between “the day after tomorrow” and “it\'s fine”; <b>なのに</b> is contrastive (“even though”), which does not fit; <b>ばかり</b> means “nothing but,” which makes no sense after a time noun.<br>Remember: <b>なら comes right after a noun to filter the options the other person just raised.</b><br>(Note: あさって<b>でも</b> だいじょうぶだよ is also natural Japanese, meaning “the day after tomorrow works too.” Both it and なら are correct, with a different nuance: なら picks the option that works, while でも concedes “that would be fine too.”)',
  'n4-own-b2-20': 'Answer: <b>なら</b>. B takes up the topic A just raised (日本語の本) and <b>gives advice about it</b>: “if it\'s Japanese books, that shop is good.”<br>Distractors: <b>だから</b> needs a cause-effect link; <b>でも</b> is contrastive; <b>まで</b> marks an end point.<br>This is another typical use of なら: <b>taking up what the other person just said and responding to it</b>. It follows the same logic as the previous question (picking one of two options): both respond to <b>something that has already been raised</b>.',
  'n4-own-b2-21': 'Answer: <b>なら</b> = “when it comes to sports.” First narrow the topic to a range, then answer within it.<br>Distractors: <b>だから</b> (“because it is sports, so...”) is illogical here; <b>でも</b> does not fit this position; <b>より</b> is for comparison (〜より = rather than...).<br>Taken together, the three questions show what なら always does: <b>it first takes up a topic or option that is already on the table, then responds</b>, whether the listener asked it, the listener raised it, or the speaker set the range.',
  'n4-own-b2-22': 'Answer: <b>の</b>. <b>When 間 follows a noun, the two are always joined with の</b>: 夏休み + <b>の</b> + 間. This is the basic rule for a noun modifying a noun, not something special about 間.<br>Distractors: <b>に / で / を</b> cannot follow a noun and then connect to 間.<br>In N5 you learned 間 as <b>space</b> (“between two things”); N4 extends it to <b>time</b> (“during”). Same word, two kinds of distance.',
  'n4-own-b2-23': 'Answer: <b>いる</b>. When a <b>verb</b> comes before 間, the form becomes <b>V-ている間</b>: 話して<b>いる</b>間 = while the teacher is talking.<br>Distractors: <b>いた</b> is past and clashes with the present request してください that follows; <b>ある</b> is for a state left by a transitive verb; <b>おく</b> means doing something in advance.<br>Learn the two patterns as a pair: <b>noun + の + 間</b> (夏休みの間) / <b>V-ている + 間</b> (話している間). <b>A noun takes の; a verb takes ている.</b>',
  'n4-own-b2-24': 'Answer: <b>の</b>. Here 間 has the N5 meaning of <b>space</b> (between two classrooms), but <b>the connection rule is exactly the same</b>: after a noun, use の.<br>Distractors: <b>に / で / から</b> cannot follow a noun and then connect to 間.<br>Put this next to question 22 and the point is clear: <b>whether 間 means space or a period of time, a noun before it always takes の</b>. The meaning changes; the connection does not.',
  'n4-own-b2-25': 'Answer: <b>切るのに</b>. Dictionary form + のに shows <b>what it costs to do something</b> (time, money, effort).<br>Each distractor matches a connection error:<br>・<b>切ったり</b> is for listing (〜たり〜たり).<br>・<b>切りに</b> is the purpose “go to do” (買いに 行く) and needs a motion verb after it.<br>・<b>切るかどうか</b> is an embedded question, “whether to cut.”<br>Key point: <b>this のに is not the contrastive “even though”; it shows use or purpose, and it always follows the dictionary form.</b>',
  'n4-own-b2-26': 'Answer: <b>のに</b>. Getting to the station takes thirty minutes; “how much it takes” is the core of this pattern.<br>Distractors: <b>ので</b> and <b>から</b> give a reason (“because I\'m going to the station, ...”), but the second half, “takes 30 minutes,” is a cost, not a result; <b>けど</b> is contrastive.<br>The clue is the ending: <b>when you see endings about cost or use, such as 〜かかります, 〜必要です or 〜便利です, the part before is usually dictionary form + のに.</b>',
  'n4-own-b2-27': 'Answer: <b>のに</b>. The question asks <b>what this tool is used for</b>; this is the “use” meaning of のに.<br>Distractors: <b>ので</b> gives a reason; <b>のが / のは</b> turn an action into a noun as subject or topic (泳ぐのが 好きです) and cannot connect to つかいますか.<br>The two common translations of this のに are two sides of the same thing: <b>“(it takes...) in order to do” and “used for doing” both describe the use or cost of the action.</b>',
  'n4-own-b2-28': 'Answer: <b>ふるかもしれません</b>. The sky has gone dark, and <b>the speaker guesses</b> it may rain.<br>The distractors all go wrong on <b>whose stance the sentence expresses</b>:<br>・<b>ふってください</b> is a request to the listener; you cannot ask rain to fall.<br>・<b>ふったほうがいいです</b> is advice to the listener.<br>・<b>ふってはいけません</b> is a prohibition.<br>All three are <b>addressed to someone else</b>; only かもしれません is <b>the speaker\'s own guess about a situation</b>.',
  'n4-own-b2-29': 'Answer: <b>おいしいかもしれません</b>. かもしれません <b>follows the plain form</b>. The plain form of an i-adjective is its dictionary form, so it attaches directly.<br>The distractors match three errors: <b>おいしいで</b> (borrowing the で of na-adjectives), <b>おいしく</b> (using the adverbial form) and <b>おいしいだ</b> (adding だ, which never follows an i-adjective).<br>How each word type connects: <b>verb</b> 降る → 降るかもしれません / <b>i-adjective</b> おいしい → おいしいかもしれません / <b>na-adjective</b> しずか → しずかかもしれません (<b>no だ</b>) / <b>noun</b> 休み → 休みかもしれません.',
  'n4-own-b2-30': 'Answer: <b>いるかもしれません</b>. B is <b>guessing the reason for the delay</b>: maybe the train is late.<br>The distractors again fail on whose stance it is: てください (request), ほうがいいです (advice) and てはいけません (prohibition) are all <b>said to the listener</b>, but here B is talking about <b>the situation of a third person who is not present</b>, so only a guess works.<br>In one line: <b>if the sentence is about how someone or something might be → かもしれません; if it is about what you should do → てください / ほうがいい.</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-01': 'Answer: <b>空きそう</b> (appearance, 様態). The first sentence describes <b>what the speaker can see</b>: the person is packing up to leave, so the speaker judges the seat is about to be free. The form is <b>masu-stem + そうです</b>: 空き<u>ます</u> → 空き<b>そう</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>空くそう</b> is <b>hearsay</b> (I hear it will be free), but here the information comes from the speaker\'s own eyes, with no source of “someone said”; <b>空きますそう</b> is a wrong connection, since そう does not attach to the full ます form; <b>空いたそう</b> means “I hear it is already free,” which contradicts “still packing.”<br>How to judge: <b>information you saw yourself → appearance (masu-stem); information someone told you → hearsay (plain form)</b>. That is why the question always gives a clue about where the information came from.',
  'n4-own-b3-02': 'Answer: <b>ふるそう</b> (hearsay). <b>〜に よると is the signature opener for hearsay</b>: the information comes from the weather forecast, not from what the speaker saw, so it takes the hearsay そう after the <b>plain form</b>: 降る + そうです.<br>Distractors: <b>ふりそう</b> is appearance (the sky looks like rain), but this sentence clearly names the forecast as the source; <b>ふりますそう</b> is a wrong connection; <b>ふったそう</b> means “I hear it rained,” which contradicts あしたは.<br>This question pairs with the previous one: <b>the two そうです look identical; you tell them apart by the connection and the source of the information.</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-03': 'Answer: <b>「見た かんじから、おいしいだろうと 思って いる」</b>. For the <b>appearance</b> そう with an i-adjective, drop the final い and add そう: おいし<u>い</u> → おいし<b>そう</b> = <b>it looks delicious</b> (not eaten yet).<br>Distractors:<br>・「人から『おいしい』と 聞いた」 is hearsay, which would be おいし<b>い</b>そうです. <b>One い makes a completely different meaning.</b><br>・「じっさいに 食べて、おいしかった」: after eating you would say おいしかったです.<br>・「これから ケーキを 作る つもりだ」 makes no sense here.<br>The two そう with i-adjectives differ by a single kana, which is where this point costs the most marks: <b>おいしそう = looks delicious</b> / <b>おいしいそう = I hear it\'s delicious</b>.',
  'n4-own-b3-04': 'Answer: <b>もらえますか</b>. The first part says <b>わたしは 今 手が はなせない</b>: the speaker cannot get away, so this is <b>asking the listener to bring the chair</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>も いいですか</b> asks permission for <b>the speaker</b> to do it, which contradicts being unable to get away; <b>いませんか</b> checks a state and is not a request; <b>は いけませんか</b> asks whether something is forbidden.<br>Two checks get you the answer: <b>(1) who does the action? (2) is the sentence a request or a check?</b> The phrase 手が はなせないので is the clue that fixes the doer as the listener.',
  'n4-own-b3-05': 'Answer: <b>あいて</b>. The direction of もらう is <b>“I receive from the other person,”</b> so the one who actually lends is <b>the listener</b>.<br>A common misreading is “may I borrow it?” That would be 借りても いいですか, where the doer is the speaker.<br>Learn as a pair:<br>・<b>〜てもらえますか</b> = could <b>you</b> do it for me? (the listener acts)<br>・<b>〜てもいいですか</b> = may <b>I</b> do it? (the speaker acts)',
  'n4-own-b3-06': 'Answer: <b>も いいですか</b>. B answers どうぞ, which <b>gives permission</b>, so A must be asking <b>“may I use it?”</b><br>Distractors: <b>もらえますか</b> and <b>くれませんか</b> both <b>ask the listener to do the action</b>, which would mean “please use this chair,” but B replies どうぞ、使って ください (offering the chair to A), the opposite direction, so it does not connect; <b>は いけませんか</b> asks “isn\'t it allowed?” a protest or a check on a ban, and does not lead naturally to どうぞ.<br><b>Order of work: read the reply first.</b> If the reply is どうぞ (permission), think of てもいいですか.',
  'n4-own-b3-07': 'Answer: <b>どこに おいたか</b>. Question word + verb + か forms a <b>noun clause</b> as one unit, embedded in a larger sentence as the object of 覚えていません.<br>Distractors:<br>・<b>どこに おいたです</b> is not a valid connection.<br>・<b>どこに おきましたか</b> is <b>a standalone question</b> and cannot be followed by a comma and another sentence.<br>・<b>どこに おいたの</b> is a casual question ending and cannot be embedded as a clause.<br>How to build it: <b>assemble the clause first (どこ + に + 置いた + か), then place it into the frame of the sentence.</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-08': 'Answer: <b>「名詞と 同じ はたらき（「知って いますか」の たいしょう）」</b>. The clause keeps the shape of a question, but in the sentence it <b>works as a noun</b>: it is <b>the thing</b> that 知っていますか asks about.<br>Distractors: calling it <b>a standalone question</b> is the most common misunderstanding; a standalone question could not be put inside another sentence as an object. Saying it <b>modifies 来る</b> confuses it with noun modification (連体修飾), which would look like 来る 時間. <b>Expressing a reason</b> has nothing to do with the structure.<br>How to check: replace the whole clause with a noun. <b>それを</b>、知っていますか also works, which shows it really is acting as a noun.',
  'n4-own-b3-09': 'Answer: <b>か</b>. An embedded question <b>must end with か</b>. It is the marker that packs the whole clause into a noun; without it the clause has no boundary.<br>Distractors: <b>が / は / を</b> mark parts of a sentence and cannot close a clause.<br>Structure: <b>[だれが パーティーに 来る<u>か</u>] + まだ わかりません</b>. Everything in the brackets is the object of わかりません.',
  'n4-own-b3-10': 'Answer: <b>の</b>. The dictionary form + の <b>turns the whole action into a noun</b>, so it can be the object of すきです.<br>Distractors: <b>を / に / で</b> cannot turn a verb into a noun; with them, nothing could serve as the subject for が すきです.<br>This の follows <b>the same logic</b> as the N5 あかい<b>の</b>を ください. It extends from standing in for a thing to turning a whole action into a thing; it is not a new rule.',
  'n4-own-b3-11': 'Answer: <b>およぐのが 上手です</b>. <b>水泳 (a Sino-Japanese action noun) = 泳ぐの (verb + の)</b>: two ways to say the same thing, and the conversion most often tested in paraphrase questions.<br>Distractors: <b>ことに</b> has the wrong particle (it must be が); <b>上手にです</b> is a wrong na-adjective form; <b>およぐの 上手です</b> is missing が.<br>When a question gives a <b>Sino-Japanese action noun</b> (水泳・勉強・運転), think of rewriting it as <b>dictionary form + の</b>.',
  'n4-own-b3-12': 'Answer: <b>じしょ形</b> (dictionary form). <b>読む</b>のが すきです (○) / 読み<b>ます</b>のが (×) / 読<b>んで</b>のが (×).<br>This is where sentence-building questions most often cost marks: the meaning is right, but a wrong connecting form still scores zero.<br>Learn the whole structure: <b>[verb dictionary form] + の + が + [すき / きらい / 上手 / 下手]</b>. The first part can be long (ピアノを ひく<u>の</u>が すきです); as long as it ends in the <b>dictionary form</b>, it is right.',
  'n4-own-b3-13': 'Answer: <b>くれた</b>. The subject is そふ<b>が</b> and the receiver is わたし<b>に</b>: <b>someone else gives to me</b>, and only くれる works in that direction.<br>Distractors: with <b>もらった</b>, に marks <b>the giver</b> (友だち<b>に</b> 本を もらった), so the sentence would mean “the camera grandpa received <b>from me</b>,” which contradicts the second half, “I still use it with care”; <b>あげた / やった</b> mean giving away, but <b>the speaker cannot be the receiver</b>, so そふが わたしに あげた is not acceptable.<br>Fix the three directions: <b>あげる = me → others</b>, <b>もらう = others → me (I am the subject)</b>, <b>くれる = others → me (the giver is the subject)</b>.',
  'n4-own-b3-14': 'Answer: <b>もらい</b>. The first part says <b>よく わからなかったので</b>: the speaker did not understand, so the speaker benefits, and the action is <b>received from the friend</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>あげ</b> would mean “I taught my friend,” which contradicts “I didn\'t understand”; with <b>くれ</b> the giver must be the subject, as in 友だち<b>が</b> 教えて くれました; <b>やり</b> is like あげ but more casual.<br>Two ways to say the same thing; the particles change with the subject:<br>・<b>私は 友だち<u>に</u> 教えて もらいました</b><br>・<b>友だち<u>が</u> 私に 教えて くれました</b><br>Both mean the same, and in both I am the one who benefits. <b>The only difference is who stands in the subject position.</b> That is why the question must give a clue about who benefits before you can choose.',
  'n4-own-b3-15': 'Answer: <b>山田さん</b>. The subject of くれる is <b>the one who does the action and gives the benefit to me</b>.<br>This tests the direction most often reversed in reading. Japanese often leaves out 私に and keeps only 山田さんが 手伝って くれました, but as soon as you see <b>くれる</b>, you know <b>the doer is the subject and the beneficiary is me</b>.<br>A habit for long passages: <b>when you see a giving or receiving verb, draw an arrow in your head</b>. The arrow of くれる always points to “me.”',
  'n4-own-b3-16': 'Answer: <b>行った ことが ない</b>. It means “have never (done),” and the form is <b>ta-form + ことがない</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>行く ことが ない</b> means “never get the chance to go / don\'t go,” which is about frequency, not experience; <b>行き ことが</b> is a wrong connection; <b>行って いる ことが ない</b> does not work.<br>The logic of the whole sentence: <b>〜たことが ないので、ぜひ〜たいです</b> = since I have never done it, I would love to try. This is the combination this pattern appears in most often.',
  'n4-own-b3-17': 'Answer: <b>食べた ことが あります</b>. To express <b>whether you have had an experience</b>, the verb before it must be in the <b>ta-form</b>.<br>Note that <b>食べる ことが あります</b> is also grammatical, but the meaning is completely different: it means <b>“I sometimes eat it”</b> (frequency), not “I have eaten it.” <b>The same ことがある means experience after the ta-form and frequency after the dictionary form.</b><br>The other two are connection errors.<br>Learn as a set: <b>ta-form + ことがある = experience</b> / <b>dictionary form + ことがある = sometimes</b>.',
  'n4-own-b3-18': 'Answer: <b>「前者は 「よていは あるが まだ」、後者は 「いままで 一度も」」</b>.<br>・<b>まだ 行って いません</b> = <b>“haven\'t gone yet”</b>; it implies “will go later” and is about progress.<br>・<b>行った ことが ありません</b> = <b>“have never once gone”</b>; it is about life experience and assumes nothing about going later.<br>The distractor 「前者が けいけん、後者が よてい」 swaps the two and is the most typical misunderstanding.<br>In one line: <b>まだ〜ていません looks at progress; 〜たことがない looks at experience.</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-19': 'Answer: <b>およげる</b>. 泳ぐ is a Group 1 verb. For the potential, <b>change the final sound from the う-row to the え-row and add る</b>: 泳<u>ぐ</u> → 泳<u>げ</u> → 泳<b>げる</b>.<br>Distractors: <b>およがれる</b> is the passive; <b>およごう</b> is the volitional (let\'s swim); <b>およがせる</b> is the causative.<br>The four changes of a Group 1 verb each land on a different row: <b>potential → え-row + る</b>, <b>passive → あ-row + れる</b>, <b>causative → あ-row + せる</b>, <b>volitional → お-row + う</b>.',
  'n4-own-b3-20': 'Answer: <b>およげる ように なりました</b>. <b>Potential form + ようになる</b> describes <b>a gradual change from “can\'t” to “can”</b>: after practicing every week, the speaker can finally swim.<br>Distractors: <b>およいで しまいました</b> means “finished swimming / ended up swimming,” with a sense of completion or regret that clashes with the achievement in やっと; <b>およぐ ことに なりました</b> is for <b>an arrangement outside your own will</b> (it was decided that you would swim), but here it is the result of your own effort; <b>およいで おきました</b> means “swam in advance.”<br>The opening まいしゅう れんしゅうして、やっと is the clue: <b>when a sentence sets up steady effort, what follows is a change in ability.</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-21': 'Answer: <b>「まえは 話せなかったが、いまは 話せる」</b>. The core of ようになる is <b>a change between before and after</b>; it always implies “it wasn\'t like this before.”<br>Distractors: <b>話すことに 決めた</b> is a decision (ことにする); <b>話さなければ ならない</b> is an obligation; <b>話したいと 思って いる</b> is a wish. None of the three has <b>the layer of “a change has happened.”</b><br>So when you see ようになりました, read not only “can do it now” but <b>“couldn\'t before, can now.”</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-22': 'Answer: <b>行ったら</b>. たら follows <b>exactly the same rules as the ta-form</b>: first make the ta-form (行く → 行っ<b>た</b>), then replace た with <b>たら</b>.<br>Each distractor matches one error: <b>行くたら</b> uses the dictionary form; <b>行きたら</b> uses the masu-stem; <b>行っだら</b> voices wrongly. た becomes だ only after two sound changes: the <b>ん sound change</b> of verbs ending in ぬ / ぶ / む (飲ん<b>だ</b>ら・遊ん<b>だ</b>ら・死ん<b>だ</b>ら), and the <b>い sound change</b> of verbs ending in ぐ (泳い<b>だ</b>ら・急い<b>だ</b>ら). After the small っ it is always た.<br>If you can make the ta-form, you do not need to learn たら separately: <b>飲む → 飲んだ → 飲んだら</b>, <b>見る → 見た → 見たら</b>, <b>する → した → したら</b>.',
  'n4-own-b3-23': 'Answer: <b>きまったら</b>. Call <b>after</b> it is decided: たら marks the <b>condition or timing</b> for the action that follows.<br>Distractors: <b>きまって</b> is a simple link and lacks the conditional sense of “after”; <b>きまるので / きまるから</b> give a reason (“because it will be decided, ...”), which is illogical: something not yet decided cannot be a reason.<br>Pattern: <b>[V-たら], [request or result]</b>. The first half is the condition; the second half happens only once the condition is met.',
  'n4-own-b3-24': 'Answer: <b>「日と 時間が きまった あとで、連絡して ほしい」</b>.<br><b>This is one of the most commonly misread types in reading comprehension.</b> The distractor 「いつ きまるのか 知りたい」 is the trap: the sentence has きまったら, and a careless reading turns it into “I want to know <b>when</b> it will be decided.” But <b>たら is not a question word; it marks the condition “after.”</b> The speaker wants to be contacted <u>after</u> it is decided, not to ask about the timing.<br>「連絡を して 日と 時間を きめたい」 reverses cause and effect (contacting in order to decide); 「連絡は しなくても いい」 is the opposite of the meaning.<br>When you read 〜たら、〜てほしい / 〜てください, ask yourself: <b>what is the condition, and what does the speaker want done once it is met?</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-25': 'Answer: <b>知らない 女の人</b>. The key is 女の人が こえを かけて <b>くれました</b>: <b>the subject of くれる is the one who does the action</b>, and the direction points to “me.”<br>This question is the heart of this point: Japanese often drops 私に and keeps only 〜が〜てくれました. <b>When you see くれる, draw an arrow pointing to “me”</b>; the subject is always where the arrow starts.',
  'n4-own-b3-26': 'Answer: <b>わたし</b>. This sentence does not drop its subject: <b>わたしは</b> お礼を 言って states it clearly.<br>Wrong answers usually come from <b>being carried along by 女の人 in the previous sentence</b> and assuming she is the subject of the whole passage.<br>When a passage has several people, build a habit: <b>label every action with who did it to whom</b> instead of sliding through on instinct. The directions in this passage: <b>女の人 → (helps) → わたし → (thanks) → 女の人</b>.',
  'n4-own-b3-27': 'Answer: <b>わたし</b>. たすかりました is <b>how the beneficiary feels</b>; only the person who was helped would say it.<br>How do you know who benefited? Look at <b>おしえて くれた</b> before it: the arrow of くれる points to “me,” so I am the one who benefited, and I am the one who is grateful.<br>Together these three questions train exactly this point: <b>the passage never once says 私に, yet the direction is clear throughout, thanks to the giving and receiving verbs, not to keywords.</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-28': 'Answer: <b>「人に しんせつに して もらった けいけんから、自分も そう したいと 思った」</b>. The <b>core event</b> of the passage is being helped by a stranger, and the <b>emotional turn</b> comes in the last sentence, 自分も 同じ ことを して あげたい. The main idea lies on the line connecting the two.<br>The distractors share one problem: <b>they grab a detail from one sentence instead of stepping back to see the whole passage</b>:<br>・「古い かさは あたらしい かさより べんりだ」: the passage never compares old and new umbrellas.<br>・「雨の 日には かさを わすれない ほうが いい」: the opening does mention forgetting an umbrella, but that is where the story starts, not its point.<br>・「店の 人は いつも わらって いる」: this does not match the text; the shop worker laughed only that one time.<br>The method for main-idea questions: <b>first state in one sentence what happened and how the feeling changed, then find the option that matches that line.</b>',
  'n4-own-b3-29': 'Answer: <b>「小さな しんせつは、人から 人へ つたわって いくのだと 思います。」</b>. A closing sentence must <b>echo the theme of the whole passage</b>: being helped → wanting to pass the kindness on. 「小さな しんせつは、人から 人へ つたわって いくのだと 思います。」 ties that line together.<br>The problem with the distractors is not grammar; <b>all four sentences are completely grammatical</b>:<br>・「つぎからは、かならず かさを 持って 出かけます。」: it only responds to the detail of forgetting an umbrella at the start and has nothing to do with the main idea.<br>・「あの 店の しなものは とても 安いです。」: the passage never mentions prices.<br>・「ちかくの 店の 場所を もっと しらべたいです。」: it has nothing to do with the feeling the passage conveys.<br>This question tests exactly <b>whether you can rule out options that make sense but are unrelated to the main idea</b>.',
  'n4-own-b3-30': 'Answer: <b>店の 人</b>. 店の 人が かさを かして <b>くれました</b>: the subject of くれる is the giver.<br>There is a reason this question sits next to the main-idea question: <b>to grasp the main idea, you first have to sort out who did what to whom</b>. If you read the direction backwards (thinking “I” lent the umbrella to the shop worker), the last sentence 自分も 同じ ことを して あげたい stops making sense, because 同じ こと refers precisely to <b>what someone else did for me</b>.<br>Sort out the directions first, then the main idea. The order cannot be reversed.',
  'n5-own-v01': 'When 「雨」 stands alone, it takes its kun\'yomi (native Japanese reading) 「あめ」. The sentence means “It is raining outside.”',
  'n5-own-v02': 'When 「空」 stands alone, it takes its kun\'yomi 「そら」. The sentence means “The sky is very blue today.”',
  'n5-own-v03': '「学校」 is a two-kanji compound, so it uses on\'yomi (Chinese-derived readings): 「がっこう」. Note that 「がく」 + 「こう」 becomes 「がっこう」 with a small っ (double consonant).',
  'n5-own-v04': '「来月」 is read with on\'yomi as 「らいげつ」. 「来」 is read 「らい」, and 「月」 is read 「げつ」 here.',
  'n5-own-v05': 'In 「半分」, the 「分」 becomes voiced as 「ぶん」 (rendaku, sequential voicing), so the whole word is read 「はんぶん」.',
  'n5-own-v06': '「火曜日」 is read 「かようび」. 「火」 takes the on\'yomi 「か」, and 「日」 becomes 「び」 through rendaku.',
  'n5-own-v07': 'In 「小さい」, the kanji 「小」 is followed by the okurigana 「さい」, giving 「ちいさい」. Note the long vowel in 「ちい」.',
  'n5-own-v08': 'The te-form of the verb 「書く（かく）」 is 「書いて（かいて）」. This is the i-sound change (イ音便).',
  'n5-own-v09': 'Here 「かわ」 means a river, and its correct kanji is 「川」. The sentence means “This town has many rivers.”',
  'n5-own-v10': '「らいねん」 means “next year,” and its correct kanji is 「来年」. 「来週」 is らいしゅう (next week), and 「来月」 is らいげつ (next month).',
  'n5-own-v11': 'The correct kanji for 「がっこう」 is 「学校」. The top of 「学」 is 「⺍」, and the right side of 「校」 is 「交」. Do not confuse them with the look-alike kanji 「宇」, 「字」 and 「枝」.',
  'n5-own-v12': 'The kanji for 「みる」 (to see, to watch) is 「見」, made of 「目」 + 「儿」. Do not confuse it with the look-alike kanji 「貝」, 「目」 and 「自」.',
  'n5-own-v13': 'The general Japanese counters go: 一つ（ひとつ）, 二つ（ふたつ）, 三つ（みっつ）, 四つ（よっつ）. 「三つ」 is read 「みっつ」, with a small っ.',
  'n5-own-v14': 'The counter for long, thin objects is 「〜本（ほん）」. After 「二」 it stays unvoiced: 「にほん」. (Compare 「一本」 いっぽん and 「三本」 さんぼん.)',
  'n5-own-v15': 'The verb in the sentence is non-past: 「あります」 (there will be a test). So the blank needs a future time word, 「あした」 (tomorrow).',
  'n5-own-v16': 'The adjective is in the past tense: 「さむかったです」 (it was cold). So choose the past time word 「きのう」 (yesterday).',
  'n5-own-v17': 'The speaker asks what time it is, so the most natural thing to look at is a 「とけい」 (clock, watch).',
  'n5-own-v18': '「傘をさす」 is a set phrase meaning “to put up an umbrella.” It is raining, so you put up an umbrella.',
  'n5-own-v19': '「AとBのあいだ」 means “between A and B.” The bookstore is between the bank and the supermarket.',
  'n5-own-v20': '「となり」 means “next door,” right beside something of the same kind. “Next to my room is Mr. Tanaka\'s room.”',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-01': 'When you talk about your own mother to others, use the humble word 「はは」. 「おかあさん」 is used for someone else\'s mother, or when you call your own mother directly.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-02': 'When you talk about someone else\'s father, use the respectful word 「おとうさん」. 「ちち」 is the humble word for your own father.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-03': '「りょうしん（両親）」 means both parents (ちちとはは). 「きょうだい」 means siblings.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-04': 'It is hot (あついです), so please open (あけて) the window. To close a window is 「しめる」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-05': '「〜てしまいました」 expresses an unfortunate or unintended action, so the answer is 「わすれて」 (forgot).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-06': 'The action you do to an apple with a knife (ナイフで) is cutting: 「きります」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-07': 'Having a question for the teacher is 「しつもん（質問）」. The sentence means “I have a question for the teacher.”',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-08': 'Studying Japanese is 「べんきょう（勉強）します」. The sentence means “I study Japanese every evening.”',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-09': 'Cleaning a room is 「そうじ（掃除）を しました」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-10': 'The subject is the speaker, and the book goes out to the friend, so the verb is 「かしました（貸しました）」 (lent). Borrowing from a friend would be 「かりました」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-11': '「から」 marks the source: 「あねから」 means “from my older sister.” Receiving something from someone uses 「もらう」, so the answer is 「もらいました」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-12': 'The subject is the friend, and the receiver is 「わたし」 (わたしに). When someone gives something to me, Japanese uses 「くれる」, so the answer is 「くれました」. 「あげる」 is for giving to someone else.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-13': 'The set verb for wind blowing is 「ふく（吹く）」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-14': 'In Japanese you literally “drink” medicine: the set phrase is 「くすりを のむ（飲む）」. Where English uses “take,” Japanese uses のむ—and 「たべる」 (eat) is never used, even for solid pills.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-15': '“To take a shower” is the set phrase 「シャワーを あびる（浴びる）」. 「する」 is not used.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-16': 'To say an item is cheap, use the i-adjective 「やすい（安い）」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-17': 'Sugar (さとう) was put in the coffee, so it is sweet (あまい). Spicy is 「からい」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-18': 'Winter (ふゆ) mornings are cold (さむい). Hot is 「あつい」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-19': 'Being close to the station makes the apartment convenient (べんり). As a na-adjective, the stem 「べんり」 comes directly before です.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-20': 'You cannot hear any sounds, so the room is quiet (しずか). Lively is 「にぎやか」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-21': '「きれい（綺麗）」 ends in the kana い, but grammatically it is a standard na-adjective (before a noun it becomes きれいな).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-22': 'The opposite of 「やすい」 (cheap) is 「たかい」 (expensive).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-23': 'The opposite of 「くらい（暗い）」 is 「あかるい（明るい）」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-24': 'The word that contrasts with 「おおきい」 (big) is 「ちいさい」 (small).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-25': 'The place where you swim (およぎます) is the loanword 「プール」 (pool).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-26': 'The loanword you can drink (のみます) is 「コーヒー」 (coffee).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-27': 'Big cities have many tall buildings, and the loanword is 「ビル」 (building).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-28': '「せんたくする（洗濯する）」 means to do the laundry, so the closest meaning is the verb 「あらう（洗う）」 (to wash).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-29': '「りょうしん（両親）」 means 「ちちと はは」 (father and mother).',
  'N5-ORIG-B2-V-30': 'Negating the opposite gives the same meaning: dark (くらい) equals not bright (あかるくない).',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-01': 'Here 「は」 marks 「わたし」 as the topic: “As for me, I like apples.” It is not a particle for place, time or means.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-02': 'The second sentence contrasts coffee with tea, so it uses the contrastive 「は」: “Tea, I don\'t drink.” Simply repeating 「を」 is not the point here.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-03': 'As a particle, 「は」 is pronounced 「わ」 (wa), even though it is written with the hiragana 「は」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-04': 'In the existence pattern 「〜に 〜があります」, the thing that exists is marked with 「が」, so the answer is 「ほんがあります」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-05': 'In the basic pattern, the object of a feeling like 「すきです」 (to like) is always marked with 「が」, so the answer is 「さかながすきです」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-06': 'When the question word 「だれ」 is the subject (asking who opened the window), it must be followed by 「が」, never 「は」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-07': '「たべます」 (to eat) is a transitive verb. The bread is its direct object, so it takes the object particle 「を」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-08': 'With 「出る」 (to leave, to go out), the place you leave from is marked with 「を」, so the answer is 「うちを出ました」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-09': 'With 「あるく」 (to walk), a space you walk through or along is marked with 「を」, so the answer is 「こうえんをあるいて」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-10': 'After a specific clock time or date with a number, use 「に」 to mark the exact time of the action, so the answer is 「6じにおきます」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-11': 'The destination of 「行きます」 is marked with 「に」, so the answer is 「きょうとにいきます」. 「らいしゅう」 is a relative time word, so it takes no 「に」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-12': 'The person you do an action toward, with verbs like 「電話をする」, 「会う」 and 「聞く」, is marked with 「に」. “I called the teacher” is 「せんせいにでんわをしました」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-13': 'Reading a book (ほんをよみます) is an action, and the place where an action happens is marked with 「で」. 「に」 is used for a place where something simply exists, or for a destination.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-14': 'A vehicle you ride is the means of travel, so it is marked with 「で」: 「バスでいきます」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-15': '“Quantity + で + price” gives the total for that quantity, so 「みっつで500えん」 means “500 yen for three.”',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-16': '「行きます」 is a verb of movement. The particle 「へ」 (pronounced e) marks the direction or destination, so “go to Japan” takes 「へ」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-17': '「帰ります」 is a verb of movement, so the direction is marked with 「へ」: 「会社へ帰ります」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-18': '「へ」 must go with a verb of movement to show direction. 「ほんがあります」 is a sentence of static existence, so its location must take 「に」, not 「へ」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-19': 'To list the two things you bought in full (bread and fruit), join the nouns with 「と」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-20': 'To say you do something together with someone, use the set pattern 「〜と いっしょに」: 「いもうとといっしょに」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-21': 'The stem stresses 「ぜんぶ」 (all of them), and a complete list must use 「と」. 「や」 gives an incomplete list (there may be other items), so the answer is 「りんごと バナナと みかんを かいました」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-22': 'The previous sentence says Mr. Tanaka drinks coffee, and the second says the same is true for the speaker: “I also drink coffee.” So 「も」 replaces 「は」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-23': 'A direct answer to 「だれが」 (who came?) focuses on the subject, so the answer uses 「が」: 「たなかさんがきました」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-24': '「も」 means the same situation as in the previous sentence applies, so 「あしたも」 means “I will work tomorrow too.” The answer is 「明日も同じように仕事をする」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-25': 'In 「わたしのかばん」 (my bag), the particle 「の」 links two nouns and shows possession.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-26': '「日本の車」 describes origin or another attribute of the noun, so the two nouns are linked with 「の」.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-27': 'In the chain of noun modifiers 「あにの ともだちの しゃしん」, 「ともだちの」 directly modifies the head noun 「しゃしん」 (photo): it is the friend\'s photo.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-28': 'The sentence has 「など」 (and so on), which clearly marks an incomplete list, so the nouns are joined with 「や」, forming the 「〜や〜など」 pattern.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-29': '「いくつかの例をあげる」 (giving a few examples) is an incomplete list, which uses the particle 「や」, as in 「りんごや バナナ」. 「と」 gives a complete list.',
  'N5-ORIG-B3-P-30': '「や」 only links nouns to give a partial list, so 「パンや ごはん」 is correct. 「パンや きれいです」 links a noun to an adjective, which breaks the rule. 「パンや たべます」 is missing the second item. In 「パンを や ごはんを たべます」, the particles are in the wrong place.',
  'n5-own-b4-01': 'When 「山」 stands alone, it takes its kun\'yomi (native Japanese reading) 「やま」. Its on\'yomi is 「サン」 (as in 富士山＝ふじさん), but as a standalone noun it uses the kun\'yomi.',
  'n5-own-b4-02': 'As a standalone position noun, 「中」 takes its kun\'yomi 「なか」. The wrong options are readings of other position nouns: そと＝外, うえ＝上, した＝下.',
  'n5-own-b4-03': '「大学」 is a Sino-Japanese compound, so both kanji use on\'yomi: だい＋がく. 「おお」 is the kun\'yomi of 「大」 (as in 大きい＝おおきい) and is not used in such compounds.',
  'n5-own-b4-04': 'Both kanji in 「電車」 use on\'yomi: でん＋しゃ. 「くるま」 is the kun\'yomi of 「車」. Sino-Japanese compounds like this normally do not mix kun\'yomi with on\'yomi.',
  'n5-own-b4-05': 'When the first sound of the second part of a compound becomes voiced, this is called rendaku (連濁): ひら＋かな → ひらがな. But かた＋かな → かたかな does not undergo rendaku. It is an exception you need to memorize.',
  'n5-own-b4-06': 'In 「て＋かみ → てがみ（手紙）」, か becomes が. This is rendaku. In やまみち, み has no voiced form, so it cannot change. あかい and たかい are adjective forms, not compounds with rendaku.',
  'n5-own-b4-07': 'The okurigana of 「食べる」 is 「べる」, because the part that changes in conjugation is written in kana. 「食る」 leaves out the べ, so the reader cannot tell whether it is たべる or くう.',
  'n5-own-b4-08': 'The okurigana of 「新しい」 starts from 「し」. A common mistake is to write 「新らしい」 with an extra ら, but the only standard spelling is 「新しい」.',
  'n5-own-b4-09': '「がっこう」＝学校. The wrong options swap in look-alike kanji: 枝 (えだ), 字 (じ), 交 (こう). They look similar but mean completely different things.',
  'n5-own-b4-10': '「みせ」＝店. The wrong options are kanji with a similar shape or the same radical: 広 (ひろい), 席 (せき), 底 (そこ).',
  'n5-own-b4-11': '「休」 is person + tree (a person resting against a tree). 「体」 is person + 本 and means body. The two kanji differ by just one horizontal stroke, which makes this the most common look-alike mistake.',
  'n5-own-b4-12': '「大」 with an extra dot is 「太」 (ふとい, thick). With an extra horizontal stroke on top, it is 「天」. With a dot at the upper right, it is 「犬」. These four kanji differ by a single stroke.',
  'n5-own-b4-13': 'Long, thin objects use 「本」, but the reading changes with the number: 1本＝いっぽん, 3本＝さんぼん, 6本＝ろっぽん. Here it is 3本, so it takes the voiced 「ぼん」. 「まい」 is for thin, flat things (paper, shirts).',
  'n5-own-b4-14': 'Thin, flat things such as 紙, 切符 and シャツ use 「枚（まい）」. 本 is for long, thin objects, 冊 for books, and 匹 for small animals. Choosing the wrong counter is a common way to lose points at N5.',
  'n5-own-b4-15': 'The day after today is 「あした」 (tomorrow). きのう＝yesterday, おととい＝the day before yesterday, まいにち＝every day (not one specific day).',
  'n5-own-b4-16': '「ひるま（昼間）」 is the daytime, between morning and night. The order of a day is 朝 → 昼 → 夕方 → 夜, so learn time words as a set.',
  'n5-own-b4-17': 'To write a letter, you use an 「えんぴつ」 (pencil). ぼうし＝hat, くつした＝socks, まど＝window. None of these is for writing.',
  'n5-own-b4-18': 'It was hot, so the thing that was opened is the 「まど」 (window). いす＝chair, ほん＝book, とけい＝clock. None of these can be opened to let air in.',
  'n5-own-b4-19': 'A place lower than the desk is 「した」 (under). うえ＝on top, なか＝inside, よこ＝beside. Learn position nouns in pairs: 上下, 中外, 前後, 左右.',
  'n5-own-b4-20': 'For something between two places, use 「〜と〜の あいだ」. となり means “next door” and refers to only one thing. まえ and うしろ mean in front and behind, not between.',
};
